00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:30.121
dwelling on the past.

1038
00:49:30.121 --> 00:49:32.460
We're supposed to be like in our bodies,

1039
00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:35.383
like our mind should be in the same place as our body.

1040
00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:41.870
And creating some space to just stop, you know.

1041
00:49:43.310 --> 00:49:44.230
It was good.

1042
00:49:45.543 --> 00:49:47.300
Have a hard time with that though.

1043
00:49:47.300 --> 00:49:49.397
We're not wired to be able to hold on to that.

1044
00:49:49.397 --> 00:49:53.070
And the half-life of those types of experiences,

1045
00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:56.236
whether it's the Loony Tunes circles closing in,

1046
00:49:56.236 --> 00:50:00.260
or the mindfulness and the presence

1047
00:50:00.260 --> 00:50:02.620
that you were able to experience

1048
00:50:02.620 --> 00:50:06.010
throughout that extended period of solitude,

1049
00:50:06.010 --> 00:50:08.250
tend to wane pretty quickly

1050
00:50:08.250 --> 00:50:10.540
in the wake of those experiences.

1051
00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:12.800
So how has that been?

1052
00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:16.620
Like, have you been able to carry that awareness

1053
00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:19.626
into your life, in the aftermath of these experiences?

1054
00:50:19.626 --> 00:50:20.459
That's the, yeah.

1055
00:50:20.459 --> 00:50:22.170
Or do you find yourself defaulting back?

1056
00:50:22.170 --> 00:50:24.560
Now you're back in LA, you're back in the studio.

1057
00:50:24.560 --> 00:50:27.820
Like how much of that are you able to hold on to?

1058
00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:29.683
That's a great question.

1059
00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:35.110
And sort of become the theme of my life,

1060
00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:38.123
at least right now, this version of Mike Posner.

1061
00:50:40.030 --> 00:50:44.070
Because I kept getting these Satori flashes of everything,

1062
00:50:44.070 --> 00:50:45.580
you know, like in the ambulance,

1063
00:50:45.580 --> 00:50:47.530
it was peace and everything made sense.

1064
00:50:50.406 --> 00:50:52.090
The summit of Everest, we can talk about that,

1065
00:50:52.090 --> 00:50:55.420
but another one of these deeply just perfect moments

1066
00:50:55.420 --> 00:50:57.370
where I'm fully present.

1067
00:50:57.370 --> 00:51:00.350
But you're right, they disappear, go away.

1068
00:51:00.350 --> 00:51:02.986
And it's like, there's gotta be a better way

1069
00:51:02.986 --> 00:51:07.350
to access that peace than to fricking almost die

1070
00:51:07.350 --> 00:51:10.540
or to risk my life climbing this insane mountain, right.

1071
00:51:10.540 --> 00:51:11.373
Like that-

1072
00:51:11.373 --> 00:51:14.400
To have to go back to the monastery.

1073
00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:15.500
That's the next one.

1074
00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:20.123
And so I actually went back to the monastery after Everest.

1075
00:51:21.650 --> 00:51:24.493
Actually it's a Buddhist center, there's no monks there.

1076
00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:29.500
And they, the place I went,

1077
00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:32.070
they have these solo cabins

1078
00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:35.050
that are set up for solitary retreats,

1079
00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:35.883
which I really liked.

1080
00:51:35.883 --> 00:51:37.960
So you go and you drop, you got this little cabin

1081
00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:40.280
with a single mattress and they go, see you in three weeks.

1082
00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:41.520
And you're just on your own.

1083
00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:44.880
Each week, they drop off a week's worth of groceries,

1084
00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:46.350
but you don't see the person.

1085
00:51:46.350 --> 00:51:47.650
It's like in a drop box.

1086
00:51:47.650 --> 00:51:51.033
You just don't, you're by yourself for three weeks.

1087
00:51:52.020 --> 00:51:57.020
And I started to realize like, basically,

1088
00:51:58.230 --> 00:52:01.803
if you can quiet the mind, right,

1089
00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:07.160
that sort of feeling of peace is just there naturally.

1090
00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:09.740
And you don't have to like have your dad die

1091
00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:11.880
or walk across America or summit Mount Everest

1092
00:52:11.880 --> 00:52:13.680
or to be at the monastery.

1093
00:52:13.680 --> 00:52:15.563
You just need to have a quiet mind.

1094
00:52:16.470 --> 00:52:21.470
And while every moment of my life

1095
00:52:22.220 --> 00:52:24.860
is not gonna have the same emotional potency

1096
00:52:24.860 --> 00:52:28.270
of finishing the walk or of almost dying and the thing

1097
00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:30.840
or being on the summit of Mount Everest, of course not.

1098
00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:35.840
Like all of my moments, I believe have the potential

1099
00:52:36.370 --> 00:52:39.230
to have an undercurrent of peace in them

1100
00:52:39.230 --> 00:52:43.890
and at least awareness, you know, being where I am.

1101
00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:48.890
And so I'm back in LA now,

1102
00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:55.840
and I'm essentially doing the exact same thing

1103
00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:58.440
I was doing before I left on the walk.

1104
00:52:58.440 --> 00:53:00.460
I go to the studio.

1105
00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:01.853
I work with artists.

1106
00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:05.190
I help them write songs.

1107
00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:07.320
I sometimes write my own songs.

1108
00:53:07.320 --> 00:53:08.523
I produce songs.

1109
00:53:09.910 --> 00:53:12.610
But everything's different on the inside.

1110
00:53:12.610 --> 00:53:15.370
You know, who I am is different.

1111
00:53:15.370 --> 00:53:20.370
And that sort of just like the marquee on the show,

1112
00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:22.700
that's like what I'm doing on the outside.

1113
00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:26.740
Well, on the inside, my practice,

1114
00:53:26.740 --> 00:53:30.390
and I mess it up more than I get it right, Rich, you know,

1115
00:53:30.390 --> 00:53:35.390
but my practice is like when I'm in the room

1116
00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:39.950
writing with someone and they're speaking,

1117
00:53:39.950 --> 00:53:41.270
I'm trying to be present.

1118
00:53:41.270 --> 00:53:43.130
That means nothing's in here.

1119
00:53:43.130 --> 00:53:44.810
I'm listening to their voice.

1120
00:53:44.810 --> 00:53:46.570
Not what the voice in here is saying

1121
00:53:46.570 --> 00:53:48.460
about what they're saying and trying.

1122
00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:52.090
Maintaining some awareness of the physical sensation

1123
00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:54.460
in my body as I'm working.

1124
00:53:54.460 --> 00:53:59.020
And yeah, there's some of that peace,

1125
00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:01.820
it's here with me, for sure, you know.

1126
00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:03.600
And sometimes easier than others.

1127
00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:05.310
Being around you, you're a peaceful guy,

1128
00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:06.530
like we connect whatever.

1129
00:54:06.530 --> 00:54:10.720
It's easier for me to do it with you, than if, whatever.

1130
00:54:10.720 --> 00:54:12.720
But that's my practice.

1131
00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:17.400
Yeah, I would imagine that's a creative superpower.

1132
00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:18.850
You know, the idea that,

1133
00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:21.050
it's calm at the bottom of the ocean

1134
00:54:21.050 --> 00:54:23.960
and then it's calm at 30,000 feet,

1135
00:54:23.960 --> 00:54:25.510
you're above the clouds, right.

1136
00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:30.510
And having that clarity or that open space

1137
00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:34.590
opens the channel for that creative instinct

1138
00:54:34.590 --> 00:54:36.800
or impulse to channel through

1139
00:54:36.800 --> 00:54:40.030
like in the moment when you're working on a song.

1140
00:54:40.030 --> 00:54:42.723
When I was at the Buddhist center,

1141
00:54:44.620 --> 00:54:48.330
I did this like, I would meditate sitting five hours a day

1142
00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.470
and I'd do like three hours of walking meditation.

1143
00:54:51.470 --> 00:54:54.800
Sounds like hard but it's awesome actually.

1144
00:54:54.800 --> 00:54:56.730
It's all relative, you walked across America,

1145
00:54:56.730 --> 00:54:57.880
so a three hour walk.

1146
00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:00.530
No, it's split up like three, one hour.

1147
00:55:00.530 --> 00:55:03.910
And like, yeah, it's actually a lot easier

1148
00:55:03.910 --> 00:55:07.000
than regular life 'cause nothing's there to upset you.

1149
00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:08.530
So if you find yourself upset-

1150
00:55:08.530 --> 00:55:10.530
Except your mind is attacking you constantly.

1151
00:55:10.530 --> 00:55:11.700
Yeah, well, if you find yourself upset,

1152
00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.080
you know I'm doing this,

1153
00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:15.870
because there's nothing wrong here.

1154
00:55:15.870 --> 00:55:18.950
I have everything and no one else is here, you know.

1155
00:55:18.950 --> 00:55:20.500
Anyways, the story I wanted-

1156
00:55:20.500 --> 00:55:23.420
The mind will search for that thing to hang its hat on.

1157
00:55:23.420 --> 00:55:24.253
Exactly.

1158
00:55:24.253 --> 00:55:25.086
To pull you out of that moment and distract you.

1159
00:55:25.086 --> 00:55:29.940
But like, I'll circle back, but you're right.

1160
00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:33.063
It will do that, the mind is clever, cunning.

1161
00:55:35.052 --> 00:55:37.230
And in my mind, like the voice,

1162
00:55:37.230 --> 00:55:38.690
which I just call it a voice in my head,

1163
00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:40.780
I nicknamed it Charlie,

1164
00:55:40.780 --> 00:55:43.750
because when in the absence of more stimuli

1165
00:55:43.750 --> 00:55:47.120
and other people speaking and all that stuff,

1166
00:55:47.120 --> 00:55:50.190
you really start to hear Charlie.

1167
00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:52.710
And like, you start to notice Charlie's trends.

1168
00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:54.890
We'll, Charlie has some patterns I've noticed.

1169
00:55:54.890 --> 00:55:59.270
One is he's frigging way more negative than I thought,

1170
00:55:59.270 --> 00:56:01.270
like a lot of doomsday scenarios.

1171
00:56:01.270 --> 00:56:02.470
Like, hey, what if I do that?

1172
00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:03.311
And then this might happen.

1173
00:56:03.311 --> 00:56:04.144
And that might happen and then, oh,

1174
00:56:04.144 --> 00:56:05.900
and then like, and then I might die.

1175
00:56:05.900 --> 00:56:08.777
And it's like, whoa, how do we get there, you know.

1176
00:56:08.777 --> 00:56:10.090
So we got that.

1177
00:56:10.090 --> 00:56:12.160
And then the other thing I noticed about Charlie

1178
00:56:12.160 --> 00:56:17.160
is he is incredibly repetitive.

1179
00:56:17.790 --> 00:56:22.790
Like the same thought over and over and over and over again.

1180
00:56:23.470 --> 00:56:26.550
It's like, I got it, man, I got it.

1181
00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:27.680
And I had this-

1182
00:56:27.680 --> 00:56:30.120
There's Charlie again, looping that thing

1183
00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:31.910
that he always talks about so annoying

1184
00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:33.620
and boring after a while.
And sometimes it's benign,

1185
00:56:33.620 --> 00:56:34.593
but sometimes it's a negative thing.

1186
00:56:34.593 --> 00:56:36.763
It's like, dude, like, all right.

1187
00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:40.000
There was one day,

1188
00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:42.253
I think it was on fifth or sixth day there.

1189
00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:46.567
I did my two hours sitting at the beginning of the day

1190
00:56:46.567 --> 00:56:49.600
and I was just like, Charlie's getting quieter and quieter.

1191
00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:51.840
And then he's just like kind of gone.

1192
00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:53.940
And then two hours ends.

1193
00:56:53.940 --> 00:56:56.840
I stand up and I realized

1194
00:56:56.840 --> 00:57:01.320
that even though I'm not sitting anymore meditating,

1195
00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:03.943
I'm still meditating and it's no Charlie.

1196
00:57:06.400 --> 00:57:11.240
And I go to make a snack.

1197
00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:14.950
I think it was, I had a piece of Wasa bread

1198
00:57:14.950 --> 00:57:17.190
and I put an avocado on it.

1199
00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:20.253
And while I made it, still, no Charlie.

1200
00:57:22.100 --> 00:57:24.560
And I closed my eyes, I took a bite

1201
00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:29.560
and it was like fireworks of flavor.

1202
00:57:29.850 --> 00:57:31.070
Like I could tell you, Rich,

1203
00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:33.610
this without reservation is the best meal

1204
00:57:33.610 --> 00:57:35.390
I've ever had in my life.

1205
00:57:35.390 --> 00:57:38.620
And the main ingredient is like quiet mind.

1206
00:57:38.620 --> 00:57:41.783
And so, I tell you this story to say,

1207
00:57:44.650 --> 00:57:49.283
when the mind is quiet, what's left.

1208
00:57:50.890 --> 00:57:53.650
It's hard to put into words what's left,

1209
00:57:53.650 --> 00:57:55.690
but it's not just like blank.

1210
00:57:55.690 --> 00:57:59.006
It's actually like, it's like,

1211
00:57:59.006 --> 00:58:01.293
forgive me for this, I can't think of a better way,

1212
00:58:01.293 --> 00:58:03.780
it's like taking the condom off life, man.

1213
00:58:03.780 --> 00:58:06.770
You're like, this is how avocado really tastes

1214
00:58:06.770 --> 00:58:09.130
when I'm not covering it up with all my judgments

1215
00:58:09.130 --> 00:58:10.812
and talking in my head and analyzing.

1216
00:58:10.812 --> 00:58:14.852
It's like, I remember putting a sock on

1217
00:58:14.852 --> 00:58:17.820
on my retreat and feeling like I was having sex,

1218
00:58:17.820 --> 00:58:19.780
like putting a sock on my foot.

1219
00:58:19.780 --> 00:58:22.960
Like all the senses are heightened

1220
00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:25.160
because it just like, we cover them up

1221
00:58:25.160 --> 00:58:28.710
with just this blabber box in our head, usually, you know.

1222
00:58:28.710 --> 00:58:33.710
And so, it turns out, I think, at least for me,

1223
00:58:34.980 --> 00:58:37.333
when we quiet Charlie,

1224
00:58:38.430 --> 00:58:40.660
it's not this like state of blankness.

1225
00:58:40.660 --> 00:58:41.953
It's like, there's a lot there.

1226
00:58:41.953 --> 00:58:43.867
Your brain comes alive.

1227
00:58:43.867 --> 00:58:44.745
Yeah.

1228
00:58:44.745 --> 00:58:45.683
Yeah, that's wow.

1229
00:58:45.683 --> 00:58:48.150
Yeah and that's what I was feeling in the ambulance

1230
00:58:48.150 --> 00:58:51.380
when I looked at that red paint and it looked brighter.

1231
00:58:51.380 --> 00:58:53.180
That's what I was feeling, you know.

1232
00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:57.270
So I think like thinking about reality

1233
00:58:57.270 --> 00:59:01.100
is like putting on real dark sunglasses, it dulls reality.

1234
00:59:01.100 --> 00:59:04.340
And presence is like taking those sunglasses off.

1235
00:59:04.340 --> 00:59:09.189
But I think that practice of naming your thinking mind

1236
00:59:09.189 --> 00:59:13.350
allows you to create that distance and that understanding

1237
00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:18.130
that there is your mind, which is good at keeping you alive

1238
00:59:18.130 --> 00:59:22.370
and doing menial tasks and sort of propelling your body

1239
00:59:23.210 --> 00:59:24.380
into forward motion.

1240
00:59:24.380 --> 00:59:26.570
But it's really not your friend

1241
00:59:26.570 --> 00:59:29.270
when it comes to so many of the important things.

1242
00:59:29.270 --> 00:59:32.630
And creating that separation allows you to understand

1243
00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:35.220
that there is a gap and a distinction

1244
00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.940
between your higher consciousness, what it feels like,

1245
00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:42.950
the experience of being truly present in your life

1246
00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:45.801
and the looping negative thought patterns

1247
00:59:45.801 --> 00:59:48.430
of that thinking brain.

1248
00:59:48.430 --> 00:59:49.263
That's right.

1249
00:59:49.263 --> 00:59:50.920
And that in truth,

1250
00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:52.571
you are that higher consciousness.

1251
00:59:52.571 --> 00:59:53.700
That's right, you are not that voice.

1252
00:59:53.700 --> 00:59:57.051
And this identifying 'cause we all over over identify

1253
00:59:57.051 --> 00:59:58.730
with the thinking brain.

1254
00:59:58.730 --> 00:59:59.563
That's right.

1255
00:59:59.563 --> 01:00:01.095
Right and we think that's who we are.

1256
01:00:01.095 --> 01:00:01.928
That's right.

1257
01:00:01.928 --> 01:00:04.390
And developing the capacity to understand

1258
01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:06.350
that that is not who we are

1259
01:00:06.350 --> 01:00:09.351
and that this is a voice that we can choose

1260
01:00:09.351 --> 01:00:12.490
to listen to or dismiss,

1261
01:00:12.490 --> 01:00:15.250
I think is a really profound practice.

1262
01:00:15.250 --> 01:00:18.287
It's deeper than you can ever imagine, right and-

1263
01:00:18.287 --> 01:00:20.260
And fricking hard.

1264
01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:22.050
Yeah and continual thing.

1265
01:00:22.050 --> 01:00:25.950
And I work on it every day.

1266
01:00:25.950 --> 01:00:30.950
But I would say I'm definitely there more

1267
01:00:31.060 --> 01:00:33.090
than I ever have been in my life.

1268
01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:35.150
And I'm not on the walk across America,

1269
01:00:35.150 --> 01:00:38.977
I'm not at the monastery, I'm living a life.

1270
01:00:38.977 --> 01:00:40.260
Yeah, you're in the world.

1271
01:00:40.260 --> 01:00:41.093
Yeah.
Yeah.

1272
01:00:41.093 --> 01:00:41.926
Yeah.

1273
01:00:41.926 --> 01:00:46.330
Yeah, it's that adage of it's easy to be serene

1274
01:00:46.330 --> 01:00:49.460
when you're in the cave, metaphorically.

1275
01:00:49.460 --> 01:00:53.110
Like you're the monk in the monastery or in the cave

1276
01:00:54.110 --> 01:00:56.450
removed from the real world.

1277
01:00:56.450 --> 01:00:58.185
That's gonna be an easier situation

1278
01:00:58.185 --> 01:01:01.160
in which to cultivate stillness and quietude.

1279
01:01:01.160 --> 01:01:04.080
But the real advanced degree,

1280
01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:06.080
is can you carry that into the world?

1281
01:01:06.080 --> 01:01:06.913
That's right.

1282
01:01:06.913 --> 01:01:09.310
And practice that when you are immersed in stimuli

1283
01:01:09.310 --> 01:01:10.840
and challenges, et cetera.

1284
01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:12.190
Yeah, that's right.

1285
01:01:12.190 --> 01:01:17.190
And when I was on retreat, I felt that.

1286
01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:19.570
It was a lot easier there.

1287
01:01:19.570 --> 01:01:23.770
It's set up to just gain a foot hold, you know.

1288
01:01:23.770 --> 01:01:27.170
I had a book while I was at the Buddhist center

1289
01:01:28.060 --> 01:01:33.060
by Thich Nhat Hanh and there was a line in it.

1290
01:01:33.230 --> 01:01:35.853
And it said, retreat is about re-entry.

1291
01:01:37.030 --> 01:01:38.910
Retreat is about re-entry.

1292
01:01:38.910 --> 01:01:40.240
So what's that mean?

1293
01:01:40.240 --> 01:01:43.770
He said that if your retreat

1294
01:01:43.770 --> 01:01:46.960
is not making you a better member of society,

1295
01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:51.960
a better brother, sister, husband, wife when you go back,

1296
01:01:52.270 --> 01:01:53.520
you're wasting your time.

1297
01:01:54.610 --> 01:01:56.663
You're not going there to like, feel,

1298
01:01:56.663 --> 01:01:58.080
this is what I used to do,

1299
01:01:58.080 --> 01:01:59.750
'cause I've been to that place three times.

1300
01:01:59.750 --> 01:02:01.920
When I was there I thought I was gonna gain

1301
01:02:01.920 --> 01:02:03.566
some super power or something,

1302
01:02:03.566 --> 01:02:06.460
or at the very least I was gonna gain an experience

1303
01:02:06.460 --> 01:02:08.240
that I could talk about

1304
01:02:08.240 --> 01:02:10.028
and tell other people I did this thing.

1305
01:02:10.028 --> 01:02:13.380
I thought I was gonna put something on.

1306
01:02:13.380 --> 01:02:15.100
Well, that's BS, man.

1307
01:02:15.100 --> 01:02:15.933
And

1308
01:02:20.060 --> 01:02:21.103
it's selfish.

1309
01:02:22.210 --> 01:02:26.440
Like when you go on retreat, you should come back better.

1310
01:02:26.440 --> 01:02:30.210
That's what the retreat is for, you know, period.

1311
01:02:30.210 --> 01:02:34.830
And when I think about that experience,

1312
01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:38.610
when I think about Everest and the walk,

1313
01:02:38.610 --> 01:02:40.980
those are all kinda retreats.

1314
01:02:40.980 --> 01:02:44.100
And so yeah, what I'm up to now,

1315
01:02:44.100 --> 01:02:47.560
is practicing that stuff internally

1316
01:02:47.560 --> 01:02:51.000
and then trying to share the stuff I learned.

1317
01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:55.403
Because those are pretty cool and privileged experiences.

1318
01:02:56.984 --> 01:02:59.330
A lot of people can't take three weeks off of work

1319
01:02:59.330 --> 01:03:03.660
and go sit alone in a cabin, or walk across America.

1320
01:03:03.660 --> 01:03:06.700
I mean, these are very unique and beautiful experiences

1321
01:03:06.700 --> 01:03:08.070
that I was blessed to have.

1322
01:03:08.070 --> 01:03:12.767
So yeah, my life now just, I go to the studio

1323
01:03:12.767 --> 01:03:15.070
and I try to be present.

1324
01:03:15.070 --> 01:03:19.200
And also I just started doing some speaking

1325
01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:22.620
and like storytelling where I share some of these stories,

1326
01:03:22.620 --> 01:03:25.435
'cause I just want to share some of this stuff I learned.

1327
01:03:25.435 --> 01:03:26.268
Yeah.

1328
01:03:26.268 --> 01:03:27.101
Yeah.

1329
01:03:27.101 --> 01:03:29.550
On the walk, and I promise we're gonna get to Everest.

1330
01:03:29.550 --> 01:03:30.990
It's all good.

1331
01:03:30.990 --> 01:03:31.823
I know.

1332
01:03:31.823 --> 01:03:32.656
It's all good.

1333
01:03:32.656 --> 01:03:34.560
One of the things that occurred to me

1334
01:03:34.560 --> 01:03:37.940
in thinking about what this was about for you,

1335
01:03:37.940 --> 01:03:41.790
and the different Mike that kind of emerged from it

1336
01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:43.740
from the Mike that began it,

1337
01:03:43.740 --> 01:03:48.300
is this arc of transcending the ego.

1338
01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:51.430
Because on some level, yes, the walk is about you,

1339
01:03:51.430 --> 01:03:53.950
but deep down, we're all egoistic

1340
01:03:53.950 --> 01:03:56.630
and you are promoting an album on some level.

1341
01:03:56.630 --> 01:03:58.540
And you're like, oh, if I do this,

1342
01:03:58.540 --> 01:04:00.540
this will actually get a bunch of attention.

1343
01:04:00.540 --> 01:04:02.490
I'll get a bunch of validation.

1344
01:04:02.490 --> 01:04:07.340
And you had this imagined vision of being joined

1345
01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:09.230
by like this huge group of people,

1346
01:04:09.230 --> 01:04:11.420
as you walked into Los Angeles.

1347
01:04:11.420 --> 01:04:13.550
And all of you together would throw this big party

1348
01:04:13.550 --> 01:04:15.167
and you'd all go into the ocean.

1349
01:04:15.167 --> 01:04:19.210
But what happens is there is this destruction

1350
01:04:19.210 --> 01:04:20.510
of the ego along the way,

1351
01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:22.480
where you're forced to kind of meet yourself

1352
01:04:22.480 --> 01:04:24.100
in a new and different way.

1353
01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:26.660
Such that when you arrive in Venice,

1354
01:04:26.660 --> 01:04:30.610
it's really about the relationship between you and you.

1355
01:04:30.610 --> 01:04:33.600
And you eschewed the temptation

1356
01:04:33.600 --> 01:04:34.990
to have a lot of people there

1357
01:04:34.990 --> 01:04:37.860
and chose to just have your closest friends

1358
01:04:37.860 --> 01:04:40.800
and to walk into the ocean by yourself.

1359
01:04:40.800 --> 01:04:45.360
And that's a really beautiful evolution,

1360
01:04:45.360 --> 01:04:47.260
I think of Mike Posner.

1361
01:04:47.260 --> 01:04:49.650
And perhaps that was something that you had

1362
01:04:49.650 --> 01:04:51.840
to contend with again during Everest.

1363
01:04:51.840 --> 01:04:55.470
And because we are all naturally egoistic, it flares up.

1364
01:04:55.470 --> 01:04:58.388
It's not like you've just transcended it.

1365
01:04:58.388 --> 01:05:03.260
And you are this person who has a public facing persona

1366
01:05:03.260 --> 01:05:05.130
and has to contend with fame

1367
01:05:05.130 --> 01:05:07.320
and everything that kinda comes with that.

1368
01:05:07.320 --> 01:05:10.580
But I think that there's something really profound

1369
01:05:10.580 --> 01:05:14.930
in that attempt, the striving to mute the ego,

1370
01:05:14.930 --> 01:05:17.218
to tap into a greater level of humility and gratitude.

1371
01:05:17.218 --> 01:05:20.220
And then just in you describing

1372
01:05:20.220 --> 01:05:23.270
how you approach your studio time now,

1373
01:05:23.270 --> 01:05:27.040
that strikes me as somebody who's kind of showing up

1374
01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:29.550
in service to these artists that you're working with.

1375
01:05:29.550 --> 01:05:30.383
Trying to be.

1376
01:05:30.383 --> 01:05:31.847
Rather than like, hey, what am I gonna extract

1377
01:05:31.847 --> 01:05:32.790
out of this experience.

1378
01:05:32.790 --> 01:05:34.830
Yeah, it is a lot different.

1379
01:05:34.830 --> 01:05:36.290
Sometimes we won't even write a song.

1380
01:05:36.290 --> 01:05:38.100
(Mike chuckles wheezily)

1381
01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:40.770
You know, sometimes I can see a person

1382
01:05:40.770 --> 01:05:44.280
is just like overwhelmed with their life.

1383
01:05:44.280 --> 01:05:46.900
I'm like, let's just go for a walk.

1384
01:05:46.900 --> 01:05:50.090
We don't have to write a song today, I don't care.

1385
01:05:50.090 --> 01:05:51.160
But yeah, man, you're right.

1386
01:05:51.160 --> 01:05:56.160
This ego thing, it's not even real, you know.

1387
01:05:57.320 --> 01:06:02.320
Like when I think about who I am, who is Mike Posner,

1388
01:06:03.070 --> 01:06:08.070
it's like this loose amalgamation of random memories

1389
01:06:08.220 --> 01:06:09.533
that aren't even all the memories, right.

1390
01:06:09.533 --> 01:06:12.700
Because I forget most of the stuff that happens to me.

1391
01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:17.190
These memories, and then like some cultural archetypes,

1392
01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:19.467
like characters for movies that I've like mixed up.

1393
01:06:19.467 --> 01:06:22.133
I'm like that's me, that's my personality.

1394
01:06:23.350 --> 01:06:24.910
And then you weave a story around that.

1395
01:06:24.910 --> 01:06:26.116
You weave a story around it.

1396
01:06:26.116 --> 01:06:27.047
Whether it's this trauma happened to me,

1397
01:06:27.047 --> 01:06:28.840
and this is why I'm this way.

1398
01:06:28.840 --> 01:06:33.840
Right, but this story slash concept slash idea,

1399
01:06:36.330 --> 01:06:40.810
I just realized one day, like it only exists in my head.

1400
01:06:40.810 --> 01:06:43.280
Like nobody else in the entire world

1401
01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:46.003
has that picture of me in their head.

1402
01:06:47.260 --> 01:06:49.070
They have, if they even know who I am,

1403
01:06:49.070 --> 01:06:50.280
they have a different picture.

1404
01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:52.137
And it's much less fleshed out

1405
01:06:52.137 --> 01:06:55.010
and they don't care as much about it.

1406
01:06:55.010 --> 01:06:57.620
That being said, none of these

1407
01:06:59.250 --> 01:07:04.250
story slash concepts slash identities in our heads are real.

1408
01:07:04.410 --> 01:07:07.000
Not like this is real, like, this is a real thing.

1409
01:07:07.000 --> 01:07:10.259
They're just like these amorphous things

1410
01:07:10.259 --> 01:07:15.259
that have no actual tie to reality.

1411
01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:21.770
And just remembering, like it always wants you to feel

1412
01:07:25.920 --> 01:07:28.920
like you're separate than it, the ego.

1413
01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:29.753
Like that's what an ego is.

1414
01:07:29.753 --> 01:07:33.060
It's like I am this thing in the world and I'm alone.

1415
01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:36.900
And, but in actuality, it's not true, of course.

1416
01:07:36.900 --> 01:07:38.160
Like we come from our parents,

1417
01:07:38.160 --> 01:07:40.410
we're attached to the planet, of course,

1418
01:07:40.410 --> 01:07:44.520
in like deeply inexplicable ways.

1419
01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:47.830
Like if the sun was a mile further back,

1420
01:07:47.830 --> 01:07:48.973
we'd all be frozen, you know.

1421
01:07:48.973 --> 01:07:51.827
Like we are connected to everything.

1422
01:07:51.827 --> 01:07:56.827
And just like remembering to make that shift,

1423
01:07:56.840 --> 01:07:58.903
life just seems a lot better,

1424
01:08:00.126 --> 01:08:02.810
when I'm living from the sort of the big place,

1425
01:08:02.810 --> 01:08:04.320
not the ego place.

1426
01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:07.050
You can't walk across America, climb Everest

1427
01:08:07.050 --> 01:08:09.450
and climb the however many mountains you climbed

1428
01:08:09.450 --> 01:08:11.380
in preparation for Everest

1429
01:08:11.380 --> 01:08:14.709
and not feel a deeper connection

1430
01:08:14.709 --> 01:08:18.870
and sense of interconnectedness with the greater whole.

1431
01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:21.690
There is some disillusion of the ego

1432
01:08:21.690 --> 01:08:24.410
that would have to take place with that,

1433
01:08:24.410 --> 01:08:27.734
because you're meeting nature on such a grand scale.

1434
01:08:27.734 --> 01:08:32.110
I think so, I think it can go either way too.

1435
01:08:32.110 --> 01:08:34.669
Like, they talk about that with psychedelics too.

1436
01:08:34.669 --> 01:08:36.100
It could fuel your ego.

1437
01:08:36.100 --> 01:08:38.070
I did all these things, this is who I am.

1438
01:08:38.070 --> 01:08:40.429
I'm Mike Posner and on my resume,

1439
01:08:40.429 --> 01:08:44.990
I'm the only Grammy nominated guy

1440
01:08:44.990 --> 01:08:47.550
who's climbed Everest and walked across America.

1441
01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:48.710
And that is who I am.

1442
01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:52.853
Yeah, yeah, it could go that way.

1443
01:08:53.700 --> 01:08:57.500
So that's like up to you, I guess, you know.

1444
01:08:57.500 --> 01:08:58.333
Up to me.

1445
01:08:59.330 --> 01:09:00.480
Right.

1446
01:09:00.480 --> 01:09:03.000
So you finish this walk.

1447
01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:04.970
Along the way, or at some point,

1448
01:09:04.970 --> 01:09:07.740
this idea of Everest occurs to you.

1449
01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:08.573
Yeah.

1450
01:09:08.573 --> 01:09:10.460
It's probably occurred to a lot of people.

1451
01:09:10.460 --> 01:09:11.293
Yeah.

1452
01:09:11.293 --> 01:09:13.970
But you decide you're actually gonna act on it.

1453
01:09:13.970 --> 01:09:18.170
Yeah, it was the snake bite over the Rockies

1454
01:09:18.170 --> 01:09:20.423
where I just thought I could do anything.

1455
01:09:21.610 --> 01:09:25.813
And that sorta dream, I dreamed about it before,

1456
01:09:25.813 --> 01:09:28.230
like I fantasized about it.

1457
01:09:28.230 --> 01:09:33.100
And it started to mutate slowly

1458
01:09:33.100 --> 01:09:36.640
from a dream into a plan and-

1459
01:09:36.640 --> 01:09:40.410
I just remember, it wasn't that long after the walk

1460
01:09:40.410 --> 01:09:42.250
where suddenly on Instagram,

1461
01:09:42.250 --> 01:09:44.003
it's like you and Colin were,

1462
01:09:44.003 --> 01:09:45.510
were you at Mount Hood or something like that.

1463
01:09:45.510 --> 01:09:47.850
You guys went on a climb.
Yeah, just two weeks later.

1464
01:09:47.850 --> 01:09:49.970
Two weeks later, 'cause I was already talking to Collin

1465
01:09:49.970 --> 01:09:51.300
while I was on the walk.

1466
01:09:51.300 --> 01:09:56.300
I said, man, is Everest as crowded as they say,

1467
01:09:56.300 --> 01:09:57.516
is it like, 'cause you know,

1468
01:09:57.516 --> 01:09:59.650
there's a lot of hoopla about Everest,

1469
01:09:59.650 --> 01:10:02.600
and misinformation and he'd been there.

1470
01:10:02.600 --> 01:10:06.630
Is it crowded, is it really, is it like, is it worth it?

1471
01:10:06.630 --> 01:10:07.760
Like, is it beautiful?

1472
01:10:07.760 --> 01:10:09.460
He was like, you know what that feeling you get

1473
01:10:09.460 --> 01:10:10.600
when you go to the Grand Canyon.

1474
01:10:10.600 --> 01:10:13.990
I said, yeah, he's like, it's like that times 100.

1475
01:10:13.990 --> 01:10:17.935
I'm like, that sounds pretty good, you know.

1476
01:10:17.935 --> 01:10:20.890
And so, he had done a lot of big projects, Colin,

1477
01:10:20.890 --> 01:10:23.680
so he kinda gave me some advice, like how to end the walk.

1478
01:10:23.680 --> 01:10:26.750
And then he said, look, do you know anything about climbing?

1479
01:10:26.750 --> 01:10:27.583
I said, nope.

1480
01:10:28.780 --> 01:10:30.050
You ever worn crampons?

1481
01:10:30.050 --> 01:10:32.300
Nope, he goes, well, why don't you come climb Mount Hood

1482
01:10:32.300 --> 01:10:33.990
with me after your walk.

1483
01:10:33.990 --> 01:10:35.620
I said, great.

1484
01:10:35.620 --> 01:10:39.200
I go, but look like, I'm really gonna come, you know,

1485
01:10:39.200 --> 01:10:40.447
like, 'cause we never met, I'm like,

1486
01:10:40.447 --> 01:10:41.940
no, I'm really gonna come.

1487
01:10:41.940 --> 01:10:42.843
He's like, okay.

1488
01:10:43.770 --> 01:10:46.250
And so yeah, two weeks after walk,

1489
01:10:46.250 --> 01:10:49.170
like I was still, still pretty darn sore,

1490
01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:52.063
but he took me up Mount Hood and we summited it.

1491
01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:55.160
And I asked him, you know,

1492
01:10:55.160 --> 01:10:57.607
if I wanna keep getting better at this, how do I do that.

1493
01:10:57.607 --> 01:11:01.503
And he goes, you should talk to Dr. Jon.

1494
01:11:02.384 --> 01:11:04.980
And he introduced me to Dr. Jon Kedrowski

1495
01:11:06.110 --> 01:11:08.680
and that's the man that became my coach

1496
01:11:08.680 --> 01:11:12.070
and my guide over the next year and a half.

1497
01:11:12.070 --> 01:11:16.400
And I just, I'm all in.

1498
01:11:16.400 --> 01:11:19.250
So I moved like basically next to Jon,

1499
01:11:19.250 --> 01:11:21.030
I got a rental house there.

1500
01:11:21.030 --> 01:11:22.000
In like Colorado?

1501
01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:23.470
In Colorado.

1502
01:11:23.470 --> 01:11:26.390
And we just fricking trained our butts off

1503
01:11:26.390 --> 01:11:27.610
the next year and a half.

1504
01:11:27.610 --> 01:11:29.050
What did that training look like?

1505
01:11:29.050 --> 01:11:31.201
Jon has a great line,

1506
01:11:31.201 --> 01:11:36.100
he says, train for climbing mountains by climbing mountains.

1507
01:11:36.100 --> 01:11:36.970
So that's what we did.

1508
01:11:36.970 --> 01:11:41.300
When we got to Everest, you sit in base camp a lot,

1509
01:11:41.300 --> 01:11:43.943
like waiting for the right weather and your body.

1510
01:11:45.040 --> 01:11:50.040
And you start to like forget you're a climber

1511
01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:52.990
because you're just sitting there waiting for so long.

1512
01:11:52.990 --> 01:11:54.930
And like my whole, the last year and a half

1513
01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:58.530
I've been climbing like every few days, like, you know and.

1514
01:11:58.530 --> 01:12:00.828
Just hitting all the fourteeners?

1515
01:12:00.828 --> 01:12:05.700
Fourteeners, we went to Ecuador,

1516
01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:06.950
climbed the volcanoes there.

1517
01:12:06.950 --> 01:12:10.070
We went to Pakistan, we went to-

1518
01:12:10.070 --> 01:12:11.720
For Pakistan, it was K2?

1519
01:12:11.720 --> 01:12:13.720
Yeah, that's a whole other thing.

1520
01:12:13.720 --> 01:12:14.650
I just went to base camp.

1521
01:12:14.650 --> 01:12:18.480
And you were there when the Nepalese crew

1522
01:12:18.480 --> 01:12:20.260
summited in the winter, right?

1523
01:12:20.260 --> 01:12:22.583
I was, I had left a few days before,

1524
01:12:24.160 --> 01:12:26.330
but yeah, I was at that base,

1525
01:12:26.330 --> 01:12:27.394
I just went to base camp.

1526
01:12:27.394 --> 01:12:28.450
So you went up to base camp, right.

1527
01:12:28.450 --> 01:12:31.546
That's it, I don't really wanna climb K2, man.

1528
01:12:31.546 --> 01:12:32.641
I always tell people that-

1529
01:12:32.641 --> 01:12:33.474
Well, not in the winter at least.

1530
01:12:33.474 --> 01:12:35.390
I don't wanna climb it in the summer either.

1531
01:12:35.390 --> 01:12:37.220
I always tell people that when I was doing Everest,

1532
01:12:37.220 --> 01:12:38.570
I'm like, look, believe it or not.

1533
01:12:38.570 --> 01:12:41.490
I do have a line, you know.

1534
01:12:41.490 --> 01:12:44.095
'Cause everyone's line of acceptable risk,

1535
01:12:44.095 --> 01:12:46.610
their risk tolerance is different.

1536
01:12:46.610 --> 01:12:50.440
And it's fine like you climb within that risk.

1537
01:12:50.440 --> 01:12:53.430
And K2 is on the other side of my line, man.

1538
01:12:53.430 --> 01:12:54.483
Forget that one.

1539
01:12:55.530 --> 01:12:58.871
But yeah, we climb.

1540
01:12:58.871 --> 01:13:00.470
And this was like full-time?

1541
01:13:00.470 --> 01:13:01.460
You're living in Colorado-

1542
01:13:01.460 --> 01:13:02.293
Full-time.

1543
