Yeah, you get there and you hear these thunder

1792
01:23:36.274 --> 01:23:40.820
(mimics avalanche rumbling) rumbles and they sound loud.

1793
01:23:40.820 --> 01:23:43.637
And you go and you look and these mountains are big, man.

1794
01:23:43.637 --> 01:23:45.000
And you're like where,

1795
01:23:45.000 --> 01:23:48.380
it sounds like it's coming down on you and you find it.

1796
01:23:48.380 --> 01:23:51.610
And it's like so far away and it looks this big,

1797
01:23:51.610 --> 01:23:53.730
you know, it's giant, but it's really far away

1798
01:23:53.730 --> 01:23:55.180
and the mountains are so big.

1799
01:23:57.370 --> 01:24:00.680
So after, we're talking like you probably hear that

1800
01:24:00.680 --> 01:24:03.133
five times a day, at least.

1801
01:24:04.570 --> 01:24:08.310
And over a period of weeks,

1802
01:24:08.310 --> 01:24:11.410
I started to become desensitized to that sound,

1803
01:24:11.410 --> 01:24:13.310
it just became kinda background noise.

1804
01:24:16.090 --> 01:24:21.023
But Everest it's really dangerous, it's really real.

1805
01:24:23.110 --> 01:24:26.540
Basically for every 100 climbers that go above base camp,

1806
01:24:26.540 --> 01:24:28.223
one perishes.

1807
01:24:29.370 --> 01:24:32.220
In fact, before we saw a weather window

1808
01:24:32.220 --> 01:24:33.993
that we liked Jon and I,

1809
01:24:37.040 --> 01:24:39.940
four climbers that weren't on our team,

1810
01:24:39.940 --> 01:24:42.040
but were in base camp with us, we're dead.

1811
01:24:44.360 --> 01:24:48.883
And so, you got guys going up, not coming down.

1812
01:24:50.600 --> 01:24:51.433
We had a friend-

1813
01:24:51.433 --> 01:24:54.450
That's some heavy shit when you're like I'm next.

1814
01:24:54.450 --> 01:24:56.320
Yeah, we had a friend that went up

1815
01:24:56.320 --> 01:24:58.693
and came down with really bad frostbite.

1816
01:25:01.240 --> 01:25:06.240
And I started thinking like, (sighs) what am I doing here.

1817
01:25:09.350 --> 01:25:13.972
Like what,

1818
01:25:13.972 --> 01:25:17.730
why am I risking my life here?

1819
01:25:17.730 --> 01:25:22.730
And all the bad reasons became really apparent, you know,

1820
01:25:22.980 --> 01:25:25.670
like there was some part of me that wanted to be cool

1821
01:25:25.670 --> 01:25:27.680
and tough and it's like, well,

1822
01:25:27.680 --> 01:25:30.480
that's a pretty dumb thing to die for, right.

1823
01:25:30.480 --> 01:25:33.600
And so all these just like reasons,

1824
01:25:33.600 --> 01:25:35.040
we were talking about reasons before,

1825
01:25:35.040 --> 01:25:37.290
like for me not to do it,

1826
01:25:37.290 --> 01:25:42.290
but just one commitment for me to climb

1827
01:25:43.160 --> 01:25:44.840
is I said I was gonna do it.

1828
01:25:44.840 --> 01:25:49.570
The follow through similar to after the snake bite.

1829
01:25:49.570 --> 01:25:51.630
That's right and-

1830
01:25:51.630 --> 01:25:55.373
What does it mean to say you're gonna do something

1831
01:25:55.373 --> 01:25:57.120
and follow through on that.

1832
01:25:57.120 --> 01:25:57.953
Yeah.

1833
01:25:57.953 --> 01:26:02.560
But then acknowledging like the ego part of it.

1834
01:26:02.560 --> 01:26:03.670
Yeah, I mean-

1835
01:26:03.670 --> 01:26:05.100
The validation part.

1836
01:26:05.100 --> 01:26:09.460
Yeah, there was a plethora of reasons of why I was there

1837
01:26:09.460 --> 01:26:11.520
and a lot of them were cool

1838
01:26:11.520 --> 01:26:16.083
and some of them weren't, which by the way,

1839
01:26:17.180 --> 01:26:18.640
I think we even talked about this last time,

1840
01:26:18.640 --> 01:26:20.030
it was true to walk too.

1841
01:26:20.030 --> 01:26:22.017
Like, you know, part of me wanted people,

1842
01:26:22.017 --> 01:26:24.080
oh, that guy's cool, you know, whatever.

1843
01:26:24.080 --> 01:26:27.660
And like, so, but that,

1844
01:26:27.660 --> 01:26:30.140
Everest was so dangerous, so real,

1845
01:26:30.140 --> 01:26:35.140
like death so close that that ceased to be,

1846
01:26:37.550 --> 01:26:40.023
the looking cool ceased to be something

1847
01:26:40.023 --> 01:26:43.100
that was pulling me upward on the mountain,

1848
01:26:43.100 --> 01:26:45.570
it became something that was pulling me down.

1849
01:26:45.570 --> 01:26:47.367
It was like, hey, man, if that's the reason you're here,

1850
01:26:47.367 --> 01:26:49.270
you should turn around, you know.

1851
01:26:49.270 --> 01:26:51.060
And it was just, the only thing I had pulling me up

1852
01:26:51.060 --> 01:26:53.170
was that commitment, man.

1853
01:26:53.170 --> 01:26:58.170
And we saw a window we liked, after, in late May.

1854
01:26:59.980 --> 01:27:03.223
And we climbed to camp two, it's about 10 hours.

1855
01:27:04.940 --> 01:27:07.440
And it was snowing hard.

1856
01:27:07.440 --> 01:27:09.290
It was like a snow storm a couple of days.

1857
01:27:09.290 --> 01:27:10.810
So we stayed at camp two.

1858
01:27:10.810 --> 01:27:13.030
But we knew there was good weather for the summit

1859
01:27:13.030 --> 01:27:15.280
in a few days, but we just had to wait.

1860
01:27:15.280 --> 01:27:20.257
And the thing about steep mountains

1861
01:27:20.257 --> 01:27:25.180
is it's not good to climb steep faces

1862
01:27:25.180 --> 01:27:26.930
the day after it snows.

1863
01:27:26.930 --> 01:27:28.980
That's when avalanches happen,

1864
01:27:28.980 --> 01:27:31.370
because it's just all this new snowpack

1865
01:27:31.370 --> 01:27:34.310
and gravity pulls it down.

1866
01:27:34.310 --> 01:27:37.210
So we're just waiting, you know,

1867
01:27:37.210 --> 01:27:40.760
after this snow storm a day or two, before we went higher,

1868
01:27:40.760 --> 01:27:42.380
because we had to go on the Lhotse face,

1869
01:27:42.380 --> 01:27:43.930
which is just, it's real steep.

1870
01:27:45.160 --> 01:27:49.900
And we're at camp two, 21,300 feet.

1871
01:27:49.900 --> 01:27:52.485
I hate that place, man, it's the worst,

1872
01:27:52.485 --> 01:27:56.395
I just feel horrible there, can't sleep.

1873
01:27:56.395 --> 01:27:58.690
And you're just there for a couple of days,

1874
01:27:58.690 --> 01:28:00.320
you're just in the tent?

1875
01:28:00.320 --> 01:28:01.190
You can get out of the tent.

1876
01:28:01.190 --> 01:28:04.080
And we had like a dining tent, which disappeared.

1877
01:28:04.080 --> 01:28:05.630
I'll get to that like (laughs).

1878
01:28:07.160 --> 01:28:12.160
Basically, yeah, I mean, you could walk to the,

1879
01:28:12.730 --> 01:28:15.060
there's like a latrine tent whatever,

1880
01:28:15.060 --> 01:28:16.830
but it's hard to do anything.

1881
01:28:16.830 --> 01:28:17.663
Right.

1882
01:28:17.663 --> 01:28:19.070
You know, you walk from where I'm sitting

1883
01:28:19.070 --> 01:28:21.238
to you're sitting, I would be breathing like this.

1884
01:28:21.238 --> 01:28:24.320
(Mike gasps for air loudly)

1885
01:28:24.320 --> 01:28:28.441
Like it's, I mean, I just, I can't explain the altitude.

1886
01:28:28.441 --> 01:28:33.287
How hard that makes everything, how bad it made me feel.

1887
01:28:33.287 --> 01:28:35.460
And it hits different people differently.

1888
01:28:35.460 --> 01:28:37.460
And that was another part of my journey.

1889
01:28:41.980 --> 01:28:46.100
I go in the tent one night at camp two with Jon,

1890
01:28:46.100 --> 01:28:49.660
Jon and I are in our tent and I'm pretending to sleep.

1891
01:28:49.660 --> 01:28:53.020
And I hear one of those rumbles.

1892
01:28:53.020 --> 01:28:54.270
I don't think anything of it

1893
01:28:54.270 --> 01:28:57.653
because I've heard it so many times by now.

1894
01:28:59.150 --> 01:29:02.825
It stops for a second and then all of a sudden,

1895
01:29:02.825 --> 01:29:06.325
(blows out air loudly) our tent starts to shake

1896
01:29:06.325 --> 01:29:08.193
from all angles, it feels like we're about to be picked up

1897
01:29:08.193 --> 01:29:09.700
and blown off the mountain.

1898
01:29:09.700 --> 01:29:11.270
A whole rips in our tent.

1899
01:29:11.270 --> 01:29:13.450
Snow's hit me in the face, filling up my sleeping bag,

1900
01:29:13.450 --> 01:29:16.552
I'm screaming, Jon, Jon, Jon, avalanche, avalanche.

1901
01:29:16.552 --> 01:29:19.920
I'm sure in that moment, I'm dead at camp two

1902
01:29:19.920 --> 01:29:21.480
the avalanche is hitting our tent.

1903
01:29:21.480 --> 01:29:22.990
And then right while I'm screaming,

1904
01:29:22.990 --> 01:29:25.640
it just fricking stopped.

1905
01:29:25.640 --> 01:29:30.640
And I'm like looking at my hands, they're still shaking.

1906
01:29:30.660 --> 01:29:34.190
And I'm like, how am I alive?

1907
01:29:34.190 --> 01:29:35.403
What happened?

1908
01:29:37.160 --> 01:29:40.780
And we figured out that basically this avalanche slid,

1909
01:29:40.780 --> 01:29:43.138
but it stopped just before hitting our camp.

1910
01:29:43.138 --> 01:29:47.830
So what we felt was the air blast

1911
01:29:47.830 --> 01:29:51.710
of the avalanche's final breaths basically.

1912
01:29:51.710 --> 01:29:53.990
But still the damage is real.

1913
01:29:53.990 --> 01:29:56.400
Like that dining tent I told you about was gone.

1914
01:29:56.400 --> 01:29:57.490
Just from the air?

1915
01:29:57.490 --> 01:29:59.753
Just like, yeah, 'cause it's so big-

1916
01:29:59.753 --> 01:30:00.664
There's so much air that it

1917
01:30:00.664 --> 01:30:02.160
displaces.
It displaces all that air.

1918
01:30:02.160 --> 01:30:03.290
Yeah, wow.
Yeah.

1919
01:30:03.290 --> 01:30:05.270
And we were lucky, man.

1920
01:30:05.270 --> 01:30:08.853
The camp next to ours, Elite Exped's, Nims' camp,

1921
01:30:10.230 --> 01:30:13.350
was like destroy and thank God they weren't there.

1922
01:30:13.350 --> 01:30:14.798
They were already at camp three.

1923
01:30:14.798 --> 01:30:16.030
They had already gone up?

1924
01:30:16.030 --> 01:30:17.121
They left the day before.

1925
01:30:17.121 --> 01:30:18.160
But they would've been toast.

1926
01:30:18.160 --> 01:30:21.380
I mean, something bad would have happened, for sure.

1927
01:30:21.380 --> 01:30:24.310
Like, it's like we didn't have time

1928
01:30:24.310 --> 01:30:25.600
to feel sorry for ourselves.

1929
01:30:25.600 --> 01:30:28.313
And still in this moment, I'm like,

1930
01:30:29.450 --> 01:30:31.479
what am I doing, you know.

1931
01:30:31.479 --> 01:30:32.490
Shit is is getting real.

1932
01:30:32.490 --> 01:30:33.860
What am I doing?

1933
01:30:33.860 --> 01:30:34.997
Do I wanna die here?

1934
01:30:34.997 --> 01:30:39.000
And still like, there's all these reasons to turn around,

1935
01:30:39.000 --> 01:30:43.820
just that commitment to integrity to pull me up.

1936
01:30:43.820 --> 01:30:46.123
And so we climb the next day to camp three.

1937
01:30:48.590 --> 01:30:51.590
Slept there, next day to camp four.

1938
01:30:51.590 --> 01:30:53.560
And then you got a few hours at camp four

1939
01:30:53.560 --> 01:30:54.713
to turn it around.

1940
01:30:55.830 --> 01:30:56.663
Right, you got like four or five hours

1941
01:30:56.663 --> 01:30:58.560
and then you gotta hit it.

1942
01:30:58.560 --> 01:31:01.380
Yeah, and it takes two hours to get ready.

1943
01:31:01.380 --> 01:31:03.460
Like getting your tank on, your big suit,

1944
01:31:03.460 --> 01:31:04.293
all that stuff.

1945
01:31:04.293 --> 01:31:05.420
And you're not really sleeping, right,

1946
01:31:05.420 --> 01:31:06.253
any of these nights?
No, that's what I'm saying,

1947
01:31:06.253 --> 01:31:08.930
I didn't really sleep well, I slept okay at camp three

1948
01:31:08.930 --> 01:31:11.350
'cause that's where I put oxygen on.

1949
01:31:11.350 --> 01:31:13.150
Camp two, I didn't sleep at all.

1950
01:31:13.150 --> 01:31:15.940
Like we were there three days, I felt just bad.

1951
01:31:15.940 --> 01:31:20.303
And then, yeah, from you leave three, you go to four,

1952
01:31:21.720 --> 01:31:24.980
I got there maybe 11:00 AM and at 6:00 PM,

1953
01:31:24.980 --> 01:31:27.195
I'm gonna start getting ready and-

1954
01:31:27.195 --> 01:31:29.200
You're hitting the supplemental oxygen

1955
01:31:29.200 --> 01:31:30.300
from that point forward?

1956
01:31:30.300 --> 01:31:32.690
Once I put it on at three, I never take it off.

1957
01:31:32.690 --> 01:31:34.430
So once you put it on, you don't take it off.

1958
01:31:34.430 --> 01:31:35.963
Sleeping with it, it's on all the time.

1959
01:31:35.963 --> 01:31:40.963
If you somehow take it off or run out,

1960
01:31:42.000 --> 01:31:44.640
that's worse than having never put it on.

1961
01:31:44.640 --> 01:31:45.560
That's really bad.

1962
01:31:45.560 --> 01:31:46.930
Because you've re-acclimated to-

1963
01:31:46.930 --> 01:31:47.837
Correct.

1964
01:31:47.837 --> 01:31:50.200
When you're doing supplemental oxygen,

1965
01:31:50.200 --> 01:31:53.313
is that like sea level oxygen density?

1966
01:31:54.278 --> 01:31:56.528
I don't know it's still really hard to like,

1967
01:31:56.528 --> 01:31:59.349
I'm still really out of breath, I think it's just-

1968
01:31:59.349 --> 01:32:01.580
So even with it, you're still struggling.

1969
01:32:01.580 --> 01:32:02.700
Oh yeah.
It's not like, oh,

1970
01:32:02.700 --> 01:32:04.287
now I feel totally normal.

1971
01:32:04.287 --> 01:32:07.380
No, no, not by any stretch of the imagination,

1972
01:32:07.380 --> 01:32:11.393
but it did help me get some, some Zs at camp three.

1973
01:32:12.790 --> 01:32:15.790
But yeah, moving real slow up there

1974
01:32:15.790 --> 01:32:17.370
and depends how much you have

1975
01:32:17.370 --> 01:32:21.357
and how many liters per minute you have it set to also.

1976
01:32:21.357 --> 01:32:23.749
Right, you open it up all the way,

1977
01:32:23.749 --> 01:32:25.282
but then you're gonna run out.

1978
01:32:25.282 --> 01:32:27.913
That's the thing, how many bottles do you have.

1979
01:32:28.800 --> 01:32:31.399
So, and that's, you gotta be responsible

1980
01:32:31.399 --> 01:32:35.860
for knowing how fast you're going as well

1981
01:32:35.860 --> 01:32:38.160
and that kind of thing.

1982
01:32:38.160 --> 01:32:42.650
So yeah, at camp four, we had that window

1983
01:32:42.650 --> 01:32:45.350
from 11:00 AM to 6:00 PM to rest.

1984
01:32:45.350 --> 01:32:49.120
I remember, this is sort of a strange story

1985
01:32:49.120 --> 01:32:50.030
I don't tell that much,

1986
01:32:50.030 --> 01:32:52.450
'cause I don't really know how to tell it that well.

1987
01:32:52.450 --> 01:32:57.450
But I like went into some strange trance,

1988
01:32:58.210 --> 01:33:00.000
almost felt like I had psychedelic.

1989
01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:02.403
I was having these visions there.

1990
01:33:03.290 --> 01:33:05.780
Granted I know the altitude had something to do with it

1991
01:33:05.780 --> 01:33:09.853
'cause we were just under 26, like 25,900.

1992
01:33:10.710 --> 01:33:12.740
And for about an hour, I was laying in the tent,

1993
01:33:12.740 --> 01:33:17.210
my eyes closed and just seeing visuals.

1994
01:33:17.210 --> 01:33:22.210
And it almost, yeah, it felt like peaceful in some way.

1995
01:33:23.795 --> 01:33:28.795
I felt a little bit removed from the experience

1996
01:33:29.133 --> 01:33:31.760
like you're always doing something

1997
01:33:31.760 --> 01:33:34.320
like managing something on the mountain.

1998
01:33:34.320 --> 01:33:36.800
And I remember that experience kind of felt

1999
01:33:36.800 --> 01:33:40.428
like it was giving me a zoom out.

2000
01:33:40.428 --> 01:33:43.300
And then it was time to go.

2001
01:33:43.300 --> 01:33:46.300
And started getting ready at six.

2002
01:33:46.300 --> 01:33:48.970
about 8:30 PM, it's dark

2003
01:33:48.970 --> 01:33:50.760
and the wind was blowing pretty good.

2004
01:33:50.760 --> 01:33:54.500
But we thought that once we got out of that saddle,

2005
01:33:54.500 --> 01:33:56.327
it would be better.

2006
01:33:56.327 --> 01:33:58.090
And I'm climbing in a team of four.

2007
01:33:58.090 --> 01:34:01.250
Dr. Jon, who we talked about, myself

2008
01:34:02.090 --> 01:34:03.530
and then our two local guides,

2009
01:34:03.530 --> 01:34:07.040
Dawa Chirring Sherpa and Dawa Dorje Sherpa.

2010
01:34:07.040 --> 01:34:09.650
And those guys are like those, my buddies, man.

2011
01:34:09.650 --> 01:34:12.290
I still talk to 'em like a lot.

2012
01:34:12.290 --> 01:34:15.530
And we start going.

2013
01:34:15.530 --> 01:34:20.450
And you're so worried about being,

2014
01:34:20.450 --> 01:34:21.990
I was so worried about being cold.

2015
01:34:21.990 --> 01:34:24.070
You hear all the stories, frostbite and stuff

2016
01:34:24.070 --> 01:34:27.127
that we're going up kinda that first face

2017
01:34:27.127 --> 01:34:31.140
and I was like sweating and you don't wanna sweat

2018
01:34:31.140 --> 01:34:33.556
on big mountains like that 'cause the sweat freezes.

2019
01:34:33.556 --> 01:34:34.701
Freezes, yeah.

2020
01:34:34.701 --> 01:34:36.060
And so.

2021
01:34:36.060 --> 01:34:37.310
And it's dark out?

2022
01:34:37.310 --> 01:34:39.930
It's dark, yeah, it's 8:30 PM when we start.

2023
01:34:39.930 --> 01:34:41.340
So we're climbing through the night

2024
01:34:41.340 --> 01:34:45.332
and our aim is to be on the summit around sunrise.

2025
01:34:45.332 --> 01:34:47.630
I started sweating and I'm like, man,

2026
01:34:47.630 --> 01:34:49.830
I gotta stop, take some layers off.

2027
01:34:49.830 --> 01:34:50.950
So I stop take a layer.

2028
01:34:50.950 --> 01:34:52.787
All these climber start passing me.

2029
01:34:52.787 --> 01:34:54.100
And that's the other thing you don't wanna

2030
01:34:54.100 --> 01:34:55.863
be in a traffic jam up there.

2031
01:34:57.890 --> 01:35:02.090
So after a few hours, there's a natural resting point

2032
01:35:02.090 --> 01:35:04.600
called the balcony where everyone takes a break

2033
01:35:04.600 --> 01:35:06.600
'cause it's just kind of flat like a balcony

2034
01:35:06.600 --> 01:35:07.950
and it has this crazy view.

2035
01:35:09.130 --> 01:35:11.680
And I said to Jon, I said, look, when we get to the balcony,

2036
01:35:11.680 --> 01:35:13.470
let's take like, everyone else is gonna

2037
01:35:13.470 --> 01:35:14.970
take a 10 minute break,

2038
01:35:14.970 --> 01:35:16.550
let's take a three minute break

2039
01:35:16.550 --> 01:35:18.330
and let's pass everybody.

2040
01:35:18.330 --> 01:35:20.090
He said, great.

2041
01:35:20.090 --> 01:35:25.090
So I get there and we take this three minute break

2042
01:35:26.047 --> 01:35:28.700
and we pass it everybody, except there's only four people

2043
01:35:28.700 --> 01:35:30.690
in front of us now.

2044
01:35:30.690 --> 01:35:35.690
And I don't usually eat caffeine,

2045
01:35:35.780 --> 01:35:38.480
but your appetite also disappears up there.

2046
01:35:38.480 --> 01:35:40.570
So I was just eating like, you know, the gels.

2047
01:35:40.570 --> 01:35:41.403
Right.

2048
01:35:41.403 --> 01:35:44.220
The caffeine, the Cliff shots.

2049
01:35:44.220 --> 01:35:47.043
And I was just like, I felt like I had-

2050
01:35:47.043 --> 01:35:47.876
Another gear.

2051
01:35:47.876 --> 01:35:51.162
Some pep in my step, unlike the days before.

2052
01:35:51.162 --> 01:35:53.588
And Dawa Chirring and I,

2053
01:35:53.588 --> 01:35:56.570
we ended up passing that group of four

2054
01:35:59.270 --> 01:36:02.400
and then there was like these two headlamps way above us.

2055
01:36:02.400 --> 01:36:04.157
So we kept climbing,

2056
01:36:04.157 --> 01:36:06.787
and we got up there on the south summit

2057
01:36:06.787 --> 01:36:07.850
and they had stopped.

2058
01:36:07.850 --> 01:36:09.200
It was our friend Erickson.

2059
01:36:10.220 --> 01:36:12.780
He was like, hey, man, I think I was going too fast.

2060
01:36:12.780 --> 01:36:14.290
I didn't wanna get there in the dark.

2061
01:36:14.290 --> 01:36:16.435
So he was just sitting there waiting.

2062
01:36:16.435 --> 01:36:20.030
And then Jon came up, he was like, nah, let's go now.

2063
01:36:20.030 --> 01:36:21.890
He's like, we're gonna hit it perfectly.

2064
01:36:21.890 --> 01:36:25.090
So we walked past Erickson and Erickson came behind us

2065
01:36:25.090 --> 01:36:30.090
and Dawa Chirring was in front of myself.

2066
01:36:30.455 --> 01:36:35.455
And I just remember like, he went and he went on the summit,

2067
01:36:40.570 --> 01:36:45.570
he turned around and he looked me in the eye.

2068
01:36:46.890 --> 01:36:50.790
And this is like this moment where you would think

2069
01:36:50.790 --> 01:36:53.480
maybe you'd feel some pride up there.

2070
01:36:53.480 --> 01:36:55.713
Like I did it, that kind of thing.

2071
01:36:56.800 --> 01:36:58.330
But I'm looking this guy in the eye,

2072
01:36:58.330 --> 01:37:01.820
who's carrying my extra oxygen, you know.

2073
01:37:01.820 --> 01:37:06.120
And it's like, nah, I'm here because of him,

2074
01:37:06.120 --> 01:37:08.140
'cause of Jon and 'cause of Dawa Dorje.

2075
01:37:08.140 --> 01:37:11.830
That's the only reason I'm allowed to be here, period.

2076
01:37:11.830 --> 01:37:13.430
Like if I had to carry all that stuff,

2077
01:37:13.430 --> 01:37:15.080
I don't think I could've done it.

2078
01:37:15.990 --> 01:37:17.400
And so instead of this pride,

2079
01:37:17.400 --> 01:37:22.400
it's just some cocktail of humility, gratitude and love.

2080
01:37:24.780 --> 01:37:27.670
And I basically hugged him.

2081
01:37:27.670 --> 01:37:30.630
Jon came up behind us and hugged me.

2082
01:37:30.630 --> 01:37:34.260
And I flopped onto the summit, man.

2083
01:37:34.260 --> 01:37:38.780
I just, I buried my face in my hands on the ground

2084
01:37:38.780 --> 01:37:42.810
and tears just explode on my face.

2085
01:37:42.810 --> 01:37:47.810
And I remember I said these word,

2086
01:37:48.970 --> 01:37:50.520
I said like, they like dribbled out.

2087
01:37:50.520 --> 01:37:54.210
I could almost hear it, I was like, this took everything,

2088
01:37:54.210 --> 01:37:55.313
This took everything.

2089
01:37:56.650 --> 01:38:00.820
And it was just such a beautiful moment, man.

2090
01:38:00.820 --> 01:38:03.380
Because Jon and I had been working so hard for it

2091
01:38:03.380 --> 01:38:04.380
to be up there with them.

2092
01:38:04.380 --> 01:38:06.810
We were both just weeping.

2093
01:38:06.810 --> 01:38:11.607
And just like in the back of the ambulance, you know,

2094
01:38:18.040 --> 01:38:22.113
it's like there was peace and everything made sense.

2095
01:38:23.570 --> 01:38:27.240
How did it differ from the many times

2096
01:38:27.240 --> 01:38:30.033
that you had imagined what that would be like?

2097
01:38:32.690 --> 01:38:35.260
Like what did you suspect it would look like

2098
01:38:35.260 --> 01:38:39.473
and feel like versus the reality?

2099
01:38:43.170 --> 01:38:47.043
That feeling of possibility that I had after the walk,

2100
01:38:48.600 --> 01:38:51.853
that wasn't there, that wasn't there.

2101
01:38:58.318 --> 01:38:59.590
In other words, that idea like,

2102
01:38:59.590 --> 01:39:03.554
after the walk, like, oh, I can, there's way more in me.

2103
01:39:03.554 --> 01:39:04.387
It wasn't-

2104
01:39:04.387 --> 01:39:06.440
But on Everest like, oh, I did everything I could,

2105
01:39:06.440 --> 01:39:08.310
but I'm only here because of these guys,

2106
01:39:08.310 --> 01:39:09.871
like that level of humility.

2107
01:39:09.871 --> 01:39:12.940
That and just like, I didn't wanna do

2108
01:39:12.940 --> 01:39:16.050
anything harder than that, I still don't.

2109
01:39:16.050 --> 01:39:17.478
Maybe I shouldn't say harder.

2110
01:39:17.478 --> 01:39:19.890
I don't wanna do anything more dangerous than that.

2111
01:39:19.890 --> 01:39:21.603
In fact, I wouldn't do that again.

2112
01:39:21.603 --> 01:39:26.603
You know, I wouldn't, my risk tolerance has changed.

2113
01:39:27.210 --> 01:39:32.210
But the moment itself was (blows out air)

2114
01:39:34.870 --> 01:39:36.570
it's one of the best moments in my life.

2115
01:39:36.570 --> 01:39:39.250
You know, it was everything and more,

2116
01:39:39.250 --> 01:39:42.070
it was just beauty, it was perfect, it's beautiful.

2117
01:39:42.070 --> 01:39:44.313
People don't realize mountains have shadows.

2118
01:39:45.350 --> 01:39:50.350
But if you get to a summit on sunrise, you see the shadow.

2119
01:39:50.360 --> 01:39:53.760
I mean, seeing Mount Everest, giant pyramidal shadow

2120
01:39:53.760 --> 01:39:56.670
cast over hundreds and hundreds of miles,

2121
01:39:56.670 --> 01:39:57.833
it's like, come on.

2122
01:39:59.087 --> 01:40:00.720
It was so clear, I mean, I saw the photos.

2123
01:40:00.720 --> 01:40:01.947
Well, clear, like, here's the thing.

2124
01:40:01.947 --> 01:40:02.843
You're so high up.

2125
01:40:02.843 --> 01:40:04.440
You got to realize you're so high,

2126
01:40:04.440 --> 01:40:05.680
you're above the clouds.

2127
01:40:05.680 --> 01:40:07.278
So, I mean, that's-

2128
01:40:07.278 --> 01:40:08.270
It's always gonna be clear up there.

2129
01:40:08.270 --> 01:40:10.360
Yeah, there's, it's not always clear.

2130
01:40:10.360 --> 01:40:11.380
It can be bad up there.

2131
01:40:11.380 --> 01:40:15.190
But on some of the weather reports,

2132
01:40:15.190 --> 01:40:18.560
like you'll have like that snow storm we had, right,

2133
01:40:18.560 --> 01:40:20.130
where we were waiting in camp two.

2134
01:40:20.130 --> 01:40:24.930
Well, that storm was, that 7,000 meters and below.

2135
01:40:24.930 --> 01:40:27.100
Like, that's where those clouds were.

2136
01:40:27.100 --> 01:40:29.300
So once we passed 7,000 meters,

2137
01:40:29.300 --> 01:40:31.260
like there was no new snowfall there.

2138
01:40:31.260 --> 01:40:32.093
We didn't have to worry.

2139
01:40:32.093 --> 01:40:33.520
You know, it's just interesting,

2140
01:40:33.520 --> 01:40:36.090
like you go above the weather to me.

2141
01:40:36.090 --> 01:40:41.090
So the moment was, I don't know what I expected,

2142
01:40:42.330 --> 01:40:46.253
but it was perfect, it was unreal, man.

2143
01:40:48.427 --> 01:40:49.663
It was everything.

2144
01:40:51.820 --> 01:40:54.560
The way that journey itself affected my life

2145
01:40:54.560 --> 01:40:56.880
was totally different than the walk.

2146
01:40:56.880 --> 01:40:58.910
You know, where the walk almost

2147
01:41:00.050 --> 01:41:05.050
like we talked about was momentum to do Everest.

2148
01:41:06.450 --> 01:41:11.450
Everest was momentum to be here, to be present.

2149
01:41:13.633 --> 01:41:16.280
I think that's a really beautiful

2150
01:41:16.280 --> 01:41:20.100
and mature takeaway from the experience,

2151
01:41:20.100 --> 01:41:24.540
because it's very easy to just let that turn you

2152
01:41:24.540 --> 01:41:26.670
into an adventure junkie,

2153
01:41:26.670 --> 01:41:30.530
where you've gotten some spike out of that experience.

2154
01:41:30.530 --> 01:41:35.260
And you then, you know, basically devote a ridiculous amount

2155
01:41:35.260 --> 01:41:37.736
of energy towards just trying to repeat that

2156
01:41:37.736 --> 01:41:40.477
or feel something again, like, you know.

2157
01:41:40.477 --> 01:41:42.437
And a lot of these guys suck, man.

2158
01:41:42.437 --> 01:41:45.210
And you're kind of like missing the point

2159
01:41:45.210 --> 01:41:47.570
because you're ultimately running away.

2160
01:41:47.570 --> 01:41:48.403
Correct.

2161
01:41:48.403 --> 01:41:50.482
Or you're escaping your reality.

2162
01:41:50.482 --> 01:41:51.315
Yeah.

2163
01:41:51.315 --> 01:41:54.000
Versus like really leveraging that experience

2164
01:41:54.000 --> 01:41:57.260
to go deeper into what you just said,

2165
01:41:57.260 --> 01:41:58.503
which is like how to be more present

2166
01:41:58.503 --> 01:42:00.137
and how to have more gratitude

2167
01:42:00.137 --> 01:42:02.403
and humility and all of these things.

2168
01:42:03.370 --> 01:42:05.500
I think we might've talked about it last time.

2169
01:42:05.500 --> 01:42:08.593
Did we talk about when I met Ram Dass?

2170
01:42:08.593 --> 01:42:09.820
I don't know, I don't remember.

2171
01:42:09.820 --> 01:42:11.510
Maybe, at the risk of repeating-

2172
01:42:11.510 --> 01:42:12.909
If I don't remember, other people don't.

2173
01:42:12.909 --> 01:42:13.992
Yeah, that's fine.

2174
01:42:13.992 --> 01:42:17.230
So I'm met Ram Dass, I don't know,

2175
01:42:17.230 --> 01:42:20.960
eight years ago before I got into all these shenanigans.

2176
01:42:20.960 --> 01:42:23.160
And I'll make this a quick story.

2177
01:42:23.160 --> 01:42:27.080
But basically I got invited to spend some time with him

2178
01:42:27.080 --> 01:42:30.763
at his house on Maui while he was still alive.

2179
01:42:31.840 --> 01:42:34.590
And I had read some of his stuff,

2180
01:42:34.590 --> 01:42:36.540
listened to some of his talks

2181
01:42:36.540 --> 01:42:39.353
and he had had a stroke by then

2182
01:42:39.353 --> 01:42:40.500
and so he was in the wheelchair.

2183
01:42:40.500 --> 01:42:44.450
And he basically said a bunch of stuff

2184
01:42:44.450 --> 01:42:47.283
I heard him say before, right.

2185
01:42:48.160 --> 01:42:50.150
But there was something like,

2186
01:42:50.150 --> 01:42:55.010
he just loved me and my friends that I was there with.

2187
01:42:55.010 --> 01:42:57.780
He didn't know us, but he was exuding this,

2188
01:42:57.780 --> 01:43:02.780
like, I don't know, like deep love for us

2189
01:43:02.980 --> 01:43:06.650
that it was like a body high or something.

2190
01:43:06.650 --> 01:43:07.780
I mean, I walked out of his house.

2191
01:43:07.780 --> 01:43:09.470
I keeled over and I was crying.

2192
01:43:09.470 --> 01:43:12.760
I felt so connected to like the rusty gate

2193
01:43:12.760 --> 01:43:15.010
and like the dirt and the cow shit.

2194
01:43:15.010 --> 01:43:18.486
Like he did something just by being him.

2195
01:43:18.486 --> 01:43:23.060
And so I thought I wanna do, like,

2196
01:43:23.060 --> 01:43:25.250
I didn't feel like I was in the presence of God

2197
01:43:25.250 --> 01:43:26.660
or anything like that, I just felt like,

2198
01:43:26.660 --> 01:43:29.290
hey, he's somebody who's done his work on himself.

2199
01:43:29.290 --> 01:43:33.220
And I thought, I wanna do that, you know.

2200
01:43:33.220 --> 01:43:36.540
I want when people are around me, then they'll feel that.

2201
01:43:36.540 --> 01:43:38.200
And I honestly, when I stop,

2202
01:43:38.200 --> 01:43:39.620
I can't really think anything

2203
01:43:39.620 --> 01:43:43.430
more important to do than that.

2204
01:43:44.624 --> 01:43:49.624
And when I'm hearing you talk about these adventure junkies,

2205
01:43:50.300 --> 01:43:55.300
like a lot of those times, those guys suck, man.

2206
01:43:55.350 --> 01:43:56.450
You're like with them and like,

2207
01:43:56.450 --> 01:44:01.450
they don't feel like how it feels

2208
01:44:01.700 --> 01:44:03.509
to be in the room with you,

2209
01:44:03.509 --> 01:44:06.950
where I feel comfortable and its just good.

2210
01:44:06.950 --> 01:44:09.150
Like you have a peaceful energy.

2211
01:44:09.150 --> 01:44:11.340
And like that's like more-

2212
01:44:11.340 --> 01:44:14.710
It comes from decades of, you know,

2213
01:44:14.710 --> 01:44:17.970
doing some work and struggling and all of that stuff.

2214
01:44:17.970 --> 01:44:20.023
I mean, I think on some level,

2215
01:44:21.500 --> 01:44:23.823
the person who is that adventure junkie

2216
01:44:23.823 --> 01:44:25.835
is seeking in their own way

2217
01:44:25.835 --> 01:44:29.860
and they're out of surface level of self discovery.

2218
01:44:29.860 --> 01:44:32.277
And hopefully, like I see the best in people

2219
01:44:32.277 --> 01:44:33.885
and that will lead them to other things-

2220
01:44:33.885 --> 01:44:35.255
Yeah, you're right, that's a better

2221
01:44:35.255 --> 01:44:36.158
way of saying it.
That will deepen

2222
01:44:36.158 --> 01:44:37.116
their trajectory.
You're right.

2223
01:44:37.116 --> 01:44:40.750
But I think if you get, it's like anything,

2224
01:44:40.750 --> 01:44:42.830
if you get stuck, then you're,

2225
01:44:42.830 --> 01:44:45.380
if you get stuck in a certain realm,

2226
01:44:45.380 --> 01:44:49.860
like you're short-cutting your growth, right.

2227
01:44:49.860 --> 01:44:53.020
Like you always have to like transcend

2228
01:44:53.020 --> 01:44:54.440
whatever experience you have.

2229
01:44:54.440 --> 01:44:55.900
Like take what you can learn from it

2230
01:44:55.900 --> 01:44:59.800
and then try to iterate on it or go to the next level.

2231
01:44:59.800 --> 01:45:02.583
I mean, there's a saying in AA,

2232
01:45:04.300 --> 01:45:06.123
you can't transmit something you haven't got, right.

2233
01:45:06.123 --> 01:45:08.320
So when you're telling the story of Ram Dass,

2234
01:45:08.320 --> 01:45:11.660
it's like that guy's gone so deep for so long,

2235
01:45:11.660 --> 01:45:15.400
and he's carrying such a heavy vibration of love

2236
01:45:15.400 --> 01:45:18.290
and wisdom and experience, that yes,

2237
01:45:18.290 --> 01:45:20.550
there is something really true and beautiful

2238
01:45:20.550 --> 01:45:22.190
and magical about being in the presence

2239
01:45:22.190 --> 01:45:25.980
of somebody who has gone that deep.

2240
01:45:25.980 --> 01:45:27.887
And you can definitely feel it, right.

2241
01:45:27.887 --> 01:45:30.550
And it's very infectious.

2242
01:45:30.550 --> 01:45:35.060
And like when you have that experience, you're like,

2243
01:45:35.060 --> 01:45:37.050
I wanna be able to, look,

2244
01:45:37.050 --> 01:45:38.580
there's a lot of charlatans out there,

2245
01:45:38.580 --> 01:45:39.970
there's a lot of people who can go,

2246
01:45:39.970 --> 01:45:42.470
anybody can go and repeat whatever Ram Dass has said

2247
01:45:42.470 --> 01:45:44.180
or their version of that.

2248
01:45:44.180 --> 01:45:46.247
But when you're with them, you can tell.

2249
01:45:46.247 --> 01:45:48.567
Like, if you're tapped in even just a little bit,

2250
01:45:48.567 --> 01:45:51.020
you can tell the difference, right,

2251
01:45:51.020 --> 01:45:53.240
between the hack and like the guy

2252
01:45:53.240 --> 01:45:54.727
who's like speaking truth-
That's right.

2253
01:45:54.727 --> 01:45:56.866
From a place of deep understanding.

2254
01:45:56.866 --> 01:45:57.699
That's right.

2255
01:45:57.699 --> 01:45:58.860
And so, yeah, for me,

2256
01:45:58.860 --> 01:46:03.792
it's like, how can I go to that place

2257
01:46:03.792 --> 01:46:07.640
so that I can transmit a vibration

2258
01:46:07.640 --> 01:46:11.090
that comes from experience, not something I read in a book.

2259
01:46:11.090 --> 01:46:12.713
Yeah, that's right.

2260
01:46:13.830 --> 01:46:16.470
Have you ever had these experiences,

2261
01:46:16.470 --> 01:46:18.840
like the opposite of the charlatan experience

2262
01:46:18.840 --> 01:46:21.010
where the charlatan is like this person dressed up

2263
01:46:21.010 --> 01:46:23.760
and they're like supposed to be like that guy,

2264
01:46:23.760 --> 01:46:25.590
and they're not?

2265
01:46:25.590 --> 01:46:27.250
Where I've had these experiences

2266
01:46:27.250 --> 01:46:30.210
where people who are not supposed to be that guy

2267
01:46:30.210 --> 01:46:33.220
or girl, or they, are.

2268
01:46:33.220 --> 01:46:34.053
Sure.

2269
01:46:34.053 --> 01:46:34.886
So for example like-

2270
01:46:34.886 --> 01:46:35.719
It's simple. It's like politicians.

2271
01:46:35.719 --> 01:46:38.363
It's like who wants that job.

2272
01:46:39.550 --> 01:46:43.960
There's an inappropriate incentive

2273
01:46:43.960 --> 01:46:46.020
that's attracting the wrong people to that role.

2274
01:46:46.020 --> 01:46:48.520
So anybody who wants to be the guru,

2275
01:46:48.520 --> 01:46:50.720
ultimately can't really be the guru.

2276
01:46:50.720 --> 01:46:53.210
Yeah, but I've had these experiences

2277
01:46:53.210 --> 01:46:55.630
where people who don't wanna be the guru

2278
01:46:55.630 --> 01:47:00.140
have guru qualities, such as like I've been in the studio

2279
01:47:01.350 --> 01:47:05.270
with rappers in clouds of marijuana smoke,

2280
01:47:05.270 --> 01:47:08.050
draped in 40 gold chains.

2281
01:47:08.050 --> 01:47:10.240
And like, I look into our eyes

2282
01:47:10.240 --> 01:47:12.322
and they're like, they're there.

2283
01:47:12.322 --> 01:47:15.400
And I'm getting like that kind of feeling from them.

2284
01:47:15.400 --> 01:47:17.871
And it's like, wow, okay.

2285
01:47:17.871 --> 01:47:18.704
It comes in all forms.
I'm pretty sure

2286
01:47:18.704 --> 01:47:19.944
you never meditated.
Yeah.

2287
01:47:19.944 --> 01:47:21.853
But well, you got it.

2288
01:47:22.690 --> 01:47:24.910
I can just tell you're present.

2289
01:47:24.910 --> 01:47:27.097
Like, when I'm speaking with you, you right here with me,

2290
01:47:27.097 --> 01:47:30.120
you're not somewhere off in the future or in the past.

2291
01:47:30.120 --> 01:47:32.454
Like I can feel it, you know,

2292
01:47:32.454 --> 01:47:36.180
I always think that's cool too

2293
01:47:36.180 --> 01:47:38.623
when that happens unexpected ones.

2294
01:47:41.420 --> 01:47:44.247
You were at base camp for a long time, man.

2295
01:47:44.247 --> 01:47:46.120
And I think, I didn't really appreciate

2296
01:47:46.120 --> 01:47:49.420
just what a huge extended endeavor

2297
01:47:49.420 --> 01:47:51.060
the whole Everest thing is.

2298
01:47:51.060 --> 01:47:51.893
Yeah.

2299
01:47:51.893 --> 01:47:54.040
Like you're out for months?

2300
01:47:54.040 --> 01:47:56.750
It was two months door to door.

2301
01:47:56.750 --> 01:47:58.150
Is that longer than normal?

2302
01:47:58.150 --> 01:48:00.010
'Cause it seemed, like you were sharing on Instagram.

2303
01:48:00.010 --> 01:48:01.260
You're like playing concerts

2304
01:48:01.260 --> 01:48:03.280
in some kind of coffee house or something.

2305
01:48:03.280 --> 01:48:04.950
I'm like, where is he?

2306
01:48:04.950 --> 01:48:05.783
It is like-

2307
01:48:05.783 --> 01:48:08.180
You're just hanging out for what seemed like forever.

2308
01:48:08.180 --> 01:48:09.300
We were, you know.

2309
01:48:09.300 --> 01:48:12.540
So below base camp there are these villages, you know,

2310
01:48:12.540 --> 01:48:15.470
'cause you hike a week to get to base camp.

2311
01:48:15.470 --> 01:48:18.180
And along the way, there's these villages

2312
01:48:18.180 --> 01:48:21.530
in the Khumbu Valley of where the Sherpa people live.

2313
01:48:21.530 --> 01:48:25.930
And if you get bored, you can go down to one of the villages

2314
01:48:25.930 --> 01:48:30.580
and be in a little lower altitude for a day.

2315
01:48:30.580 --> 01:48:35.580
Anyways, there was a hurricane I think,

2316
01:48:36.740 --> 01:48:39.183
or typhoon or something in the Indian Ocean.

2317
01:48:40.220 --> 01:48:43.470
And that was causing the jet stream

2318
01:48:43.470 --> 01:48:46.910
to stay on Everest longer than usual.

2319
01:48:46.910 --> 01:48:49.870
Now the jet stream hits Everest summit

2320
01:48:49.870 --> 01:48:52.860
usually 50 weeks out of the year.

2321
01:48:52.860 --> 01:48:54.560
So that's pretty much unclimbable,

2322
01:48:55.660 --> 01:48:59.037
at least from my risk tolerance, you know.

2323
01:48:59.037 --> 01:49:02.040
And we're talking hundreds of mile an hour winds.

2324
01:49:02.040 --> 01:49:03.260
You don't wanna be up there.

2325
01:49:03.260 --> 01:49:06.860
And usually in late May when the seasons switches

2326
01:49:06.860 --> 01:49:11.070
from spring to the monsoon season,

2327
01:49:11.070 --> 01:49:14.930
there's these two weeks where the jet stream

2328
01:49:14.930 --> 01:49:17.430
gets pushed off the mountain.

2329
01:49:17.430 --> 01:49:21.087
And this hurricane just messed it up

2330
01:49:21.087 --> 01:49:23.580
and it was just staying on there longer than usual.

2331
01:49:23.580 --> 01:49:27.313
So we had an extremely late summit June 1st.

2332
01:49:28.839 --> 01:49:31.843
So yeah, we were there a little longer than we thought.

2333
01:49:33.240 --> 01:49:36.480
And you know, that creates some other problems

2334
01:49:36.480 --> 01:49:39.910
like lower on the mountain because it's hot,

2335
01:49:39.910 --> 01:49:43.300
it starts getting hotter and the anchors and screws

2336
01:49:43.300 --> 01:49:45.220
can start to get a little loose and fall out.

2337
01:49:45.220 --> 01:49:48.830
And the hotter it is, the more stuff falls.

2338
01:49:48.830 --> 01:49:53.830
So I mean, after that close call in camp two,

2339
01:49:56.130 --> 01:49:58.432
that sound wasn't background noise anymore to me.

2340
01:49:58.432 --> 01:49:59.265
Right.

2341
01:49:59.265 --> 01:50:02.200
And so we just kept hearing them.

2342
01:50:02.200 --> 01:50:06.350
And after we summited we get down to camp two

2343
01:50:06.350 --> 01:50:08.300
really compressing it.

2344
01:50:08.300 --> 01:50:10.170
Well, that's a trip like the false finish.

2345
01:50:10.170 --> 01:50:11.460
Wanna talk about false finish line,

2346
01:50:11.460 --> 01:50:13.100
you summit, but you're only a camp two.

2347
01:50:13.100 --> 01:50:15.180
You got to go down through the ice fall which is-

2348
01:50:15.180 --> 01:50:16.770
It's like eight hours up,

2349
01:50:16.770 --> 01:50:19.180
but then 12 hours down or something like that?

2350
01:50:19.180 --> 01:50:23.913
Yeah, it's about a 17 hour day.

2351
01:50:24.750 --> 01:50:28.750
So 'cause we started that day at four,

2352
01:50:28.750 --> 01:50:30.900
summited came down and two.

2353
01:50:30.900 --> 01:50:33.570
But you go to bed and you summited Everest,

2354
01:50:33.570 --> 01:50:35.777
but you're not done, you're still fricking on Everest.

2355
01:50:35.777 --> 01:50:37.930
And actually the most dangerous part of the mountain

2356
01:50:37.930 --> 01:50:40.870
statistically is the lowest part, the Khumbu Icefall.

2357
01:50:40.870 --> 01:50:44.730
That's where all the crevasses are and the ladders

2358
01:50:44.730 --> 01:50:46.440
and that kind of thing, you still gotta do that.

2359
01:50:46.440 --> 01:50:47.752
I didn't realize those were so low.

2360
01:50:47.752 --> 01:50:50.760
That's the first thing you do on the south side.

2361
01:50:50.760 --> 01:50:52.523
If you climb from the north from Tibet.

2362
01:50:52.523 --> 01:50:53.670
Those look terrifying, I've seen all the videos.

2363
01:50:53.670 --> 01:50:54.960
Yes.

2364
01:50:54.960 --> 01:50:55.830
It's like I want

2365
01:50:55.830 --> 01:50:56.870
no part of that.
It's ridiculous.

2366
01:50:56.870 --> 01:50:58.640
We had practiced the heck out of that too

2367
01:50:58.640 --> 01:51:00.970
on ladders and stuff, so I was pretty countable.

2368
01:51:00.970 --> 01:51:02.410
But it's just interesting,

2369
01:51:02.410 --> 01:51:05.100
like you practice on all these ladders in America

2370
01:51:05.100 --> 01:51:06.900
and you get there and you're wearing crampons.

2371
01:51:06.900 --> 01:51:08.789
You get there and like the steps are all smaller.

2372
01:51:08.789 --> 01:51:12.733
So its like it doesn't fit the same way anyway.

2373
01:51:13.890 --> 01:51:16.197
And Colin and Jenna were up there too right?

2374
01:51:16.197 --> 01:51:17.030
Yeah, they went on the

2375
01:51:17.030 --> 01:51:17.987
same day as us.
So I thought maybe

2376
01:51:17.987 --> 01:51:20.320
you went, you all went together,

2377
01:51:20.320 --> 01:51:22.590
but they went up on a different day.

2378
01:51:22.590 --> 01:51:23.640
No, same day.

2379
01:51:23.640 --> 01:51:24.473
Oh, they did

2380
01:51:24.473 --> 01:51:25.820
Yeah, same day.
Just later in the day.

2381
01:51:25.820 --> 01:51:28.570
They left a few hours after us, I think.

2382
01:51:28.570 --> 01:51:31.410
But once you get to camp for we just lost contact with them,

2383
01:51:31.410 --> 01:51:33.350
I mean, the wind is howling.

2384
01:51:33.350 --> 01:51:35.483
We didn't know what tent what persons in.

2385
01:51:36.330 --> 01:51:38.980
But we summited and then when I was coming down,

2386
01:51:38.980 --> 01:51:39.910
I saw him coming up.

2387
01:51:39.910 --> 01:51:44.910
And I saw Colin and Jenna and they were pretty much there.

2388
01:51:45.810 --> 01:51:47.243
I knew they were gonna make it.

2389
01:51:47.243 --> 01:51:48.237
It's pretty cool to see them.

2390
01:51:48.237 --> 01:51:50.303
That's like a cool moment because you're like-

2391
01:51:50.303 --> 01:51:51.136
He has thing.

2392
01:51:51.136 --> 01:51:54.630
That's the guy I was talking to on the walk across America

2393
01:51:54.630 --> 01:51:55.520
and like actually here we are right now.

2394
01:51:55.520 --> 01:51:56.933
Well, he will helped me so much,

2395
01:51:56.933 --> 01:51:58.500
gave me so much advice.

2396
01:51:58.500 --> 01:52:02.370
But he does this joke with me because like,

2397
01:52:02.370 --> 01:52:03.810
I'm this musician, right.

2398
01:52:03.810 --> 01:52:07.040
You know, I'm out like in these crazy places with Jon.

2399
01:52:07.040 --> 01:52:09.850
And so anytime like I'm in a place that I guess,

2400
01:52:09.850 --> 01:52:11.960
like I on paper, shouldn't be like,

2401
01:52:11.960 --> 01:52:14.520
I land in the Skardu Airport in Pakistan.

2402
01:52:14.520 --> 01:52:16.830
He always comes up and he's like, he goes,

2403
01:52:16.830 --> 01:52:20.948
oh shit, is that Mike Posner in the Skardu Airport.

2404
01:52:20.948 --> 01:52:21.917
I'm like, what's up, man.

2405
01:52:21.917 --> 01:52:24.837
Then like he's always doing these, like in the grand,

2406
01:52:24.837 --> 01:52:26.700
oh shit, is that Mike Posner

2407
01:52:26.700 --> 01:52:29.390
on the summit of the Grand Teton in Wyoming,

2408
01:52:29.390 --> 01:52:31.141
you know, he's got these like foldered.

2409
01:52:31.141 --> 01:52:33.270
And then he did the one.

2410
01:52:33.270 --> 01:52:35.420
And actually it was just like, really cool.

2411
01:52:36.640 --> 01:52:38.360
Like in that moment where we crossed,

2412
01:52:38.360 --> 01:52:39.390
we're on the Hillary Step.

2413
01:52:39.390 --> 01:52:41.230
Holy shit.
I don't know,

2414
01:52:41.230 --> 01:52:42.649
he didn't have his phone, I think he had

2415
01:52:42.649 --> 01:52:43.563
the GoPro or something.

2416
01:52:43.563 --> 01:52:46.460
He's like, oh shit, is that Mike Pose

2417
01:52:46.460 --> 01:52:48.907
on the Hillary Step of Mount Everest.

2418
01:52:48.907 --> 01:52:50.700
I'm like, yeah.

2419
01:52:50.700 --> 01:52:52.620
You should do an edit all of those together.

2420
01:52:52.620 --> 01:52:54.390
We should, man, we should.

2421
01:52:54.390 --> 01:52:55.860
Wow, man.

2422
01:52:55.860 --> 01:52:59.700
So here you are in the wake of all of these experiences.

2423
01:52:59.700 --> 01:53:03.493
Like, how do you, like, what's the, like,

2424
01:53:04.750 --> 01:53:06.212
how do you make sense of all of it?

2425
01:53:06.212 --> 01:53:11.212
What is the wisdom that you then carry into the studio?

2426
01:53:12.830 --> 01:53:13.680
Like, how does this,

2427
01:53:13.680 --> 01:53:17.857
I'm interested in how this all congeals with

2428
01:53:19.270 --> 01:53:24.270
and works with the art and your expression.

2429
01:53:24.443 --> 01:53:26.120
That's the easy part.

2430
01:53:26.120 --> 01:53:28.490
I mean, that just happens.

2431
01:53:28.490 --> 01:53:31.310
Quincy Jones said the deeper the human you are,

2432
01:53:31.310 --> 01:53:33.670
the deeper the musician you are.

2433
01:53:33.670 --> 01:53:34.573
It's true.

2434
01:53:37.780 --> 01:53:42.780
So my perspective is totally different, you know.

2435
01:53:42.840 --> 01:53:44.840
So where I write from is different.

2436
01:53:44.840 --> 01:53:46.910
I don't have to try to do that, you know,

2437
01:53:46.910 --> 01:53:50.320
the inspiration just comes up and

2438
01:53:55.410 --> 01:53:57.377
it'll just be different and it'll be evolved.

2439
01:53:57.377 --> 01:53:59.160
Right, but it gives you something to say.

2440
01:53:59.160 --> 01:54:01.150
I mean, you can't be an artist

2441
01:54:01.150 --> 01:54:03.070
if you're not living your life.

2442
01:54:03.070 --> 01:54:04.275
So if you just sat

2443
01:54:04.275 --> 01:54:05.660
in a studio-
That's right.

2444
01:54:05.660 --> 01:54:09.370
In Hollywood for the last four years,

2445
01:54:09.370 --> 01:54:12.050
you could create technically good music,

2446
01:54:12.050 --> 01:54:16.880
but it would be devoid of the depth

2447
01:54:16.880 --> 01:54:19.440
that you can give it now that you've lived

2448
01:54:19.440 --> 01:54:21.250
like these rich experiences.

2449
01:54:21.250 --> 01:54:23.760
Yes and there's a balance, right.

2450
01:54:23.760 --> 01:54:27.110
Because there's skills involved in what I do

2451
01:54:30.040 --> 01:54:31.860
that I wasn't focusing on.

2452
01:54:31.860 --> 01:54:33.250
And in some way, some of those things

2453
01:54:33.250 --> 01:54:34.570
probably waned a little bit.

2454
01:54:34.570 --> 01:54:37.383
My guitar chops are not as, you know.

2455
01:54:39.930 --> 01:54:41.440
But you're right, I've had periods of my life

2456
01:54:41.440 --> 01:54:43.150
where it was the opposite,

2457
01:54:43.150 --> 01:54:46.720
where I was just writing a song every day.

2458
01:54:46.720 --> 01:54:49.900
And after a year of that, I'm writing some song about,

2459
01:54:49.900 --> 01:54:52.760
I don't know, in my early 20s I'm writing some songs

2460
01:54:52.760 --> 01:54:54.540
about like being at a party.

2461
01:54:54.540 --> 01:54:55.960
And I'm realizing like,

2462
01:54:55.960 --> 01:54:58.030
when's the last time you've been to a party, man,

2463
01:54:58.030 --> 01:55:01.163
you don't do that, you just write, that's all you do.

2464
01:55:03.940 --> 01:55:06.110
And I always talk about this experience I had

2465
01:55:07.840 --> 01:55:10.620
with "Redemption Song", Bob Marley.

2466
01:55:10.620 --> 01:55:11.820
That's my favorite song.

2467
01:55:13.540 --> 01:55:17.690
I'm listening to it one day and I'm thinking,

2468
01:55:17.690 --> 01:55:22.690
I know all the chords in this song, I can hit all the notes

2469
01:55:24.853 --> 01:55:29.853
and all the words in the lyrics are in my vocabulary.

2470
01:55:31.530 --> 01:55:35.383
So why haven't I written a song this good?

2471
01:55:36.460 --> 01:55:38.580
Well, that delta between wherever I am

2472
01:55:38.580 --> 01:55:40.632
and wherever "Redemption Song" is,

2473
01:55:40.632 --> 01:55:44.700
is not found in the studio.

2474
01:55:44.700 --> 01:55:48.200
It's not found by practicing guitar, you know,

2475
01:55:48.200 --> 01:55:50.650
making my voice better in voice lessons

2476
01:55:50.650 --> 01:55:54.250
or learning new words by reading.

2477
01:55:54.250 --> 01:55:56.180
It's something deeper than that.

2478
01:55:56.180 --> 01:56:00.953
And I think that's part of the quest I've been on.

2479
01:56:03.550 --> 01:56:08.550
Yeah, you gotta have a deep reservoir of life experience

2480
01:56:10.600 --> 01:56:14.380
to get to a place where you have something to say

2481
01:56:14.380 --> 01:56:16.930
worthy of a song like that

2482
01:56:16.930 --> 01:56:20.893
and worthy of you being a steward of that expression.

2483
01:56:21.730 --> 01:56:24.280
And when the universe decides that you're ready,

2484
01:56:24.280 --> 01:56:26.898
I have no doubt that it'll just flow out of you-

2485
01:56:26.898 --> 01:56:27.731
Thank you.

2486
01:56:27.731 --> 01:56:30.530
Without thinking without, what's it, Charlie.

2487
01:56:30.530 --> 01:56:31.363
Charlie.

2488
01:56:31.363 --> 01:56:32.196
Without Charlie getting involved.

2489
01:56:32.196 --> 01:56:34.308
Charlie's not gonna have any part in that one.

2490
01:56:34.308 --> 01:56:35.437
(both chuckling lightly)

2491
01:56:35.437 --> 01:56:36.896
But you came out of the gate

2492
01:56:36.896 --> 01:56:41.510
with this freaking catchy tune, "Mama Always Told Me."

2493
01:56:41.510 --> 01:56:42.343
Yes.
I can't get that

2494
01:56:42.343 --> 01:56:43.570
out of my head.
Hey, hey.

2495
01:56:43.570 --> 01:56:44.450
I don't know what it is,

2496
01:56:44.450 --> 01:56:47.450
but you have a knack for like writing these songs

2497
01:56:47.450 --> 01:56:51.080
that just get lodged in the brain like that-

2498
01:56:51.080 --> 01:56:51.913
Thank you.

2499
01:56:51.913 --> 01:56:54.620
That like pop sensibility, you know.

2500
01:56:54.620 --> 01:56:55.690
I don't know where I got that.

2501
01:56:55.690 --> 01:56:57.250
Do you think about that or is it just,

2502
01:56:57.250 --> 01:56:59.130
I'm just doing what I feel?

2503
01:56:59.130 --> 01:57:01.822
Or do you think, I know that these

2504
01:57:01.822 --> 01:57:06.540
are characteristics of a pop song that work

2505
01:57:06.540 --> 01:57:11.140
and so I structure my lyrics or my beats around that?

2506
01:57:11.140 --> 01:57:12.690
Like how does it come together?

2507
01:57:13.610 --> 01:57:17.740
You try not to think about that too much, you know.

2508
01:57:17.740 --> 01:57:20.800
But there is a frame, like there is a medium.

2509
01:57:20.800 --> 01:57:23.280
We call it a medium of the song.

2510
01:57:23.280 --> 01:57:25.200
What, it's about three minutes,

2511
01:57:25.200 --> 01:57:27.957
it's shrinking as our intention spans shrink,

2512
01:57:27.957 --> 01:57:30.083
songs are actually becoming shorter.

2513
01:57:32.091 --> 01:57:35.490
And there's like a typical structure to the medium,

2514
01:57:35.490 --> 01:57:38.880
which is, there's a verse, a pre-chorus,

2515
01:57:38.880 --> 01:57:42.860
a chorus, another verse, another pre-chorus, another chorus,

2516
01:57:42.860 --> 01:57:45.870
maybe a post-chorus, a bridge, one more chorus.

2517
01:57:45.870 --> 01:57:47.990
And that's the end of the song.

2518
01:57:47.990 --> 01:57:51.421
And so we play with this form,

2519
01:57:51.421 --> 01:57:52.740
I play with this form.
Right, in the way

2520
01:57:52.740 --> 01:57:56.240
a painter plays with a canvas that's mounted on a frame.

2521
01:57:56.240 --> 01:58:00.180
And sometimes we say, hey, we're not gonna do it.

2522
01:58:00.180 --> 01:58:02.150
Maybe this song is just gonna have one verse

2523
01:58:02.150 --> 01:58:02.983
or, you know, whatever.

2524
01:58:02.983 --> 01:58:04.260
We're not going to use that.

2525
01:58:04.260 --> 01:58:07.437
And but the medium exists, you know, before me.

2526
01:58:07.437 --> 01:58:09.690
And so I'm toying with it.

2527
01:58:09.690 --> 01:58:14.280
And it's all about what you're trying to accomplish.

2528
01:58:14.280 --> 01:58:17.290
And, you know, I guess what the Spirit is calling you to do.

2529
01:58:17.290 --> 01:58:22.290
Sometimes you'd think of a melody that's extremely catchy,

2530
01:58:22.310 --> 01:58:25.910
but it's like almost so catchy, it's kinda corny.

2531
01:58:25.910 --> 01:58:29.463
And then you're kinda, I don't, that doesn't fit, you know.

2532
01:58:30.760 --> 01:58:34.260
So it's just, it's always different.

2533
01:58:34.260 --> 01:58:35.950
Yeah.
Yeah.

2534
01:58:35.950 --> 01:58:38.110
The music industry to seems

2535
01:58:38.110 --> 01:58:40.470
to be always changing so quickly.

2536
01:58:40.470 --> 01:58:42.090
I mean, it's changed so much just since

2537
01:58:42.090 --> 01:58:44.026
the last time that we talked.

2538
01:58:44.026 --> 01:58:45.609
Oh yeah, oh yeah.

2539
01:58:46.650 --> 01:58:50.300
Now it's about TikTok and having your songs trend there.

2540
01:58:50.300 --> 01:58:53.060
And I know you've had some success with that.

2541
01:58:53.060 --> 01:58:55.490
But the idea of like, I'm gonna release an album

2542
01:58:55.490 --> 01:58:57.945
versus I'm going to drip songs out.

2543
01:58:57.945 --> 01:59:02.800
How do you even approach it strategically to think,

2544
01:59:02.800 --> 01:59:06.380
you know, thinking about like, this is how you do it now,

2545
01:59:06.380 --> 01:59:10.280
or this is the way the music kind of environment functions

2546
01:59:10.280 --> 01:59:13.340
